hadiths[605]{hadithnumber,text}:
"2421","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: A man came to Ibn Abbas and said: I prayed with so-and-so and he said twenty-two takbirs, as if intending to fault him by that; Ibn Abbas said: Woe to you! That is the sunnah of Abu al-Qasim."
"2422","From Israil, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Maymun, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab used to complete the takbir in prayer."
"2423","From Mamar: Adi ibn Artah ordered al-Hasan to lead the people in prayer, so he said this takbir when lowering and when raising; the people erred, so he said with them the takbir of the imams of that day."
"2424","From Jafar ibn Sulayman, from a man called Musa, he said: I heard al-Hasan, when a man said to him: O Abu Said, we have an imam who says the takbir in prayer when he raises and when he lowers; al-Hasan said: By Him besides Whom there is no god, it is indeed the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2425","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, he said: We were discussing the addition of this takbir in prayer, and Abu al-Shatha said: I prayed behind Ibn Abbas and did not hear him say it."
"2426","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Awn ibn Abdullah, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz said to me: Are Umar and Ibn Umar just in your view? He said: I said yes. He said: They did not use to say this takbir."
"2427","From Ismail ibn Abdullah Abu al-Walid, from Ibn Awn, he said: Qasim ibn Muhammad prayed the Maghrib, leading us in it, and did not say this takbir when raising and when prostrating. When I finished I said to him: Nafi informed me that he prayed behind Abu Hurayrah and he said the takbir when raising and when prostrating. He said: He became angry and said: May you have no father! Do you think it is binding upon me to do everything Abu Hurayrah used to do? Did you not ask him whether Abdullah ibn Umar used to do it? So I asked Nafi, and he said: No one who understands prayer has abandoned it."
"2428","From Ismail also, he said: Shubah ibn al-Hajjaj informed me, from a man, from Ibn Abza, from his father: Umar ibn al-Khattab led them and did not say this takbir."
"2429","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Do we say the takbir in voluntary prayer like we say it in the obligatory? He said: Yes, make the voluntary like the obligatory if you can in all of that; it is only something by which you seek the Face of Allah and the Hereafter."
"2430","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Sabit informed me that the proper form of prayer is that a man say the takbir with his hands and his face and in it, and raise his head a little when he begins, when he bows, and when he raises his head."
"2431","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Nafi: Did you ever see Abdullah ibn Umar, when he said the takbir in prayer, raising his head and face toward the sky? He said: Yes, a little."
"2432","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to raise his hands when saying the takbir until they were level with his shoulders, or close to that; and when he raised his head from the bow he raised them, and he did not do that in prostration."
"2433","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab told me, from Salim ibn Abdullah: Ibn Umar used to say: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood for prayer, he raised his hands until they were level with his shoulders, then said the takbir; and when he intended to bow he did the same, and when he raised from bowing he did the same; and he did not do it when raising his head from prostration."
"2434","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, he said: When Ibn Umar stood for prayer he raised his hands until they were level with his shoulders; when he bowed he raised them; when he raised his head from the bow he raised them; when he stood from the pair he raised them; and he did not do that in prostration. He said: Then he would inform them that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to do it. Abdullah said: I heard Nafi narrating from Ibn Umar the same, except he said: He raises his hands until they are level with his ears."
"2435","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi informed me that Ibn Umar used to say the takbir with his hands when he began, when he bowed, when he said: Allah hears the one who praises Him, when he raised his head from the bow, and when he stood up from the pair. He said: And he did not say the takbir with his hands when he raised his head from the two prostrations. I said to Nafi: Did Ibn Umar make the first of them the highest? He said: No, equal. I said: Did he bring his ears behind with any of them? He said: No, nor reaching his face; and he pointed to me toward the breasts or below them."
"2436","From Mamar, from Qatadah: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to raise his hands when he bowed and when he raised his head from bowing until they were level with his ears."
"2437","From al-Thawri, from Asim ibn Kulayb, from his father, from Wail ibn Hujr, he said: I watched the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): he raised his hands in prayer when he said the takbir, then when he said the takbir he raised his hands, then when he said: Allah hears the one who praises Him, he raised. He said: Then he sat, spreading his left foot, then placed his left hand on his left knee and his right forearm on his right thigh, then pointed with his forefinger, placing the thumb on the middle, forming a ring with it, and clenching the rest of his fingers; then he prostrated, and his hands were level with his ears."
"2438","From Hushaym, he said: Abu Hamzah, the freedman of Banu Asad, informed me, he said: I saw Ibn Abbas, when he began prayer, raise his hands, and when he bowed, and when he raised his head from bowing."
"2439","From Dawud ibn Ibrahim, he said: I saw Wahb ibn Munabbih, when he said the takbir in prayer, raise his hands until they were level with his ears, and when he bowed, and when he raised his head from bowing."
"2440","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Hasan ibn Muslim informed me, he said: I heard Tawus, when asked about raising the hands in prayer, saying: I saw Abdullah and Abdullah and Abdullah raising their hands in prayer - Abdullah ibn Umar, Abdullah ibn Abbas, and Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr."
"2441","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Hasan ibn Muslim informed me, from Tawus, that he said: The first takbirah, that of the opening, with the hands, is higher than the rest of the takbir. He said: Until he brings his head behind with it. Ibn Jurayj said: I myself saw Ibn Tawus bringing his head behind with his hands."
"2442","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: I have seen you saying the takbir with your hand when you begin, when you bow, when you raise your head from the first prostration and from the last, and when you stand up from the pair. He said: Yes. I said: Has it reached you that the opening takbir with the hands is greater than the rest? He said: No. I said: Does he bring the ears behind with the hands? He said: No. He said: It has reached me that from Uthman, that he used to bring his ears behind with his hands. From Ibn Jurayj he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr mentioning that from Uthman."
"2443","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: And in voluntary prayer, of the hands, the same as in the obligatory? He said: Yes, in every prayer."
"2444","From al-Thawri, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from al-Bara ibn Azib, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said the takbir, he raised his hands until his thumbs were seen close to his ears. From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yazid, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from al-Bara ibn Azib, the same, and he added, he said: Once, then do not repeat raising them in that prayer."
"2445","From al-Thawri, from al-Zubayr ibn Adi, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad: Umar ibn al-Khattab used to raise his hands to the shoulders."
"2446","From al-Thawri, from Husayn, from Ibrahim, from Ibn Masud: he used to raise his hands at the first, then not raise afterwards. From Ibn Uyaynah, from Husayn, from Ibrahim, from Ibn Masud, the same."
"2447","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, he said: I asked Ibrahim about that? He said: He raises his hands the first time."
"2448","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if I forget to say the takbir with my hand in some of that - shall I repeat the prayer? He said: No."
"2449","From Mamar, he said: I asked Hammad about a man who forgets the opening takbirah? He said: He repeats his prayer."
"2450","From Hammad, he said: When a man forgets the takbirah that opens the prayer, he repeats the prayer; and al-Thawri holds this."
"2451","From al-Thawri, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyyah, from Ali, raised to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The key of prayer is purification; its entering is the takbir, and its exit is the taslim."
"2452","From Uthman ibn Matar, from Husayn al-Muallim, from Budayl al-Uqati, from Abu al-Jawza, he said: I heard Aishah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to open his prayer with the takbir and seal it with the taslim."
"2453","From Mamar, he said: I heard Ibrahim and Qatadah, about a man who forgets the takbirah that opens the prayer: they said: He does not repeat; he has said the takbir when bowing and when prostrating."
"2454","From al-Thawri, from al-Hakam and Ata, they said: The takbirah of the bow suffices him."
"2455","From Ibn Jurayj: A man said to Ata: I forgot the takbir - shall I repeat? He said: No; you say the takbir when you sit and in between that; you only repeat if you forget a unit or a prostration."
"2456","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if I forget some of the takbir - shall I utter it with my mouth? He said: Do not repeat, and do not prostrate the prostration of forgetfulness; you will say the takbir."
"2457","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Saib ibn Umayr informed me, he said: I said to Ibn al-Musayyab: I prostrate on Friday and Satan says to me: You did not say the opening takbirah. Ibn al-Musayyab said: You said the takbir before and after."
"2458","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If you stand straight in the row and do not say the takbir until the imam bows and raises his head from the bow, then bow and count it; but if you had not stood straight in the row, do not count it."
"2459","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Layla, from al-Hakam, he said: If a man forgets to say the takbir in prayer and says: Glory be to Allah, it suffices him to open with the remembrance of Allah."
"2460","From al-Thawri, he said: If a man says the takbir before the imam, let him repeat the takbir; and if he does not repeat until he finishes the prayer, let him repeat the prayer."
"2461","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I said to him: If it seems to me that the imam has said the opening takbirah and I say the takbir, then say the takbir afterwards? He said: Say the takbir along with him."
"2462","From al-Thawri, he said: I heard - or one who heard him narrating informed me - from Hammad, he said: I asked Ibrahim: When does the imam say the takbir - when the muadhdhin finishes or before he finishes? He said: Whichever of those you do, there is no harm."
"2463","He said: And al-Amash informed me, from Ibrahim: that he used to say the takbir when the muadhdhin said: The prayer has started."
"2464","From Hushaym, from al-Mughirah, from Ibrahim: that he said the takbir once when the muadhdhin said: The prayer has started."
"2465","From Yahya ibn al-Ala, from Mughirah, he said: I said to Ibrahim: When the muadhdhin says the prayer has started, shall I say the takbir on the spot, or when he finishes? He said: Whichever you wish. He said: And Ibrahim said: The takbir is decisive; he says: It is not prolonged."
"2466","From Ja'far ibn Sulayman, from Ali ibn Ali al-Rifa'i, from Abu al-Mutawakkil al-Naji, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rose at night and began his prayer, he said the takbir, then said: Glory be to You, O Allah, and with Your praise; blessed is Your name, exalted is Your majesty, and there is no god but You; then he would declare Allah's oneness three times and magnify Him three times, then say: I seek refuge with Allah, the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing, from the accursed Satan."
"2467","From al-Muthanna ibn al-Sabbah, he said: Ikrimah ibn Khalid informed me that Umar used to teach the people that when a man stands for prayer he should say before the recitation: Glory be to You, O Allah, and with Your praise; blessed is Your name, exalted is Your majesty; there is no god but You. From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from Umar the like of it."
"2468","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, who said: When Umar began the prayer he would say: Glory be to You, O Allah, and with Your praise; blessed is Your name, exalted is Your majesty, and there is no god but You."
"2469","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: One whom I trust narrated to me, from Abu Bakr, and from Umar, and from Uthman, and from Ibn Mas'ud, that when they began they would say: Glory be to You, O Allah, and with Your praise; blessed is Your name, exalted is Your majesty, and there is no god but You."
"2470","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from a man, from Ibn Umar, who said: A man came while the people were in prayer, and when he reached the row he said: Allah is Most Great with much greatness, much praise belongs to Allah, and glory be to Allah morning and evening. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished the prayer he said: Who is the author of the words? The man said: I am, O Messenger of Allah; by Allah I intended nothing by them but good. He said: I saw the gates of heaven opened for them. Ibn Umar said: I have not abandoned them since I heard them."
"2471","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Haytham ibn Hanash, that he saw Ibn Umar and prayed with him at his side, and he said: Allah is Most Great, Allah is Most Great with much greatness; much praise belongs to Allah; glory be to Allah morning and evening. O Allah, make You the most beloved thing to me and the best thing with me."
"2472","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Humayd al-Tawil, from Anas ibn Malik, who said: A man entered while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in his prayer, and he was out of breath; when he entered he said: Much praise to Allah, blessed and good. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished his prayer he said: Who is the author of the words? twice. A man said: I am, O Messenger of Allah. He said: I saw them with twelve angels racing as to which of them would precede with them in greeting Allah, the Blessed and Exalted. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Why do I hear your breathing? He said: The iqamah was called so I hurried. He said: When you hear the iqamah, walk in your calm state; what you catch, pray, and what you miss, make up."
"2473","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, who said: I heard Abu Hurayrah say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you rises at night, let him begin his prayer with two light rak'ahs. Hisham said: Muhammad used to recite in the first of them: O you who believe, spend of what We have provided you before a day comes in which there is no trade nor friendship, up to: they will abide, and in the other: To Allah belongs what is in the heavens and what is on earth; whether you disclose what is in yourselves or conceal it, Allah will reckon you for it; He forgives whom He wills and punishes whom He wills; Allah is over all things Powerful, to the end of the surah."
"2474","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Rabi'ah ibn Ka'b al-Aslami, who said: I used to sleep in the chamber of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I would hear him when he rose at night praying, saying: Praise belongs to Allah, Lord of the worlds, for a long while (al-hawiyy), then saying: Glory be to Allah, the Magnificent, and with His praise, for a long while. I said to him: What is al-hawiyy? He said: He supplicates for a while."
"2475","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sulayman al-Ahwal informed me that Tawus informed him that he heard Ibn Abbas say: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prostrated at night, he would say: O Allah, to You belongs praise; You are the Light of the heavens and the earth; to You belongs praise, You are the Sustainer of the heavens and the earth and whoever is in them; to You belongs praise, You are the Lord of the heavens and the earth. You are the Truth, Your promise is truth, meeting You is truth, Your word is truth, Paradise is truth, Hell is truth, the prophets are truth, and the Hour is truth. O Allah, to You I have submitted, in You I have believed, upon You I have relied, to You I have turned, by You I have disputed, to You I have referred judgment; so forgive me what I have done before and after, what I have hidden and declared. You are my God; there is no god but You."
"2476","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Sulayman al-Ahwal, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, who said: When he rose at night, he would say: O Allah, to You belongs praise; to You belongs the dominion of the heavens and the earth; to You belongs praise, You are the Custodian of the heavens and the earth and what is in them; to You belongs praise, to You belongs the dominion of the heavens and the earth and whoever is in them. You are the Truth, Your promise is truth, meeting You is truth, Paradise is truth, Hell is truth, the prophets are truth, Muhammad is truth, and the Hour is truth. O Allah, to You I have submitted, in You I have believed, upon You I have relied, to You I have turned, by You I have disputed, to You I have referred judgment; so forgive me what I have done before and after, hidden and declared. You are the Advancer and You are the Delayer; there is no god but You."
"2477","From al-Hasan ibn Umarah, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damrah, who said: When Ali began the prayer, he would say: Allah is Most Great; there is no god but You, glory be to You. I have wronged myself, so forgive me; none forgives sins but You. At Your service and blessedness; good is in Your hands, and evil is not attributed to You. Guided is whom You guide; Your servant is before You, Your servant is before You; from You and to You; no refuge nor escape from You but to You. Blessed and exalted are You; glory be to You, Lord of the House."
"2478","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Abdullah ibn al-Fadl, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi', from Ali, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood for the prescribed prayer, he said the takbir and raised his hands level with his shoulders, then said: I have turned my face to the One who originated the heavens and the earth, inclining to truth, the verse and the two verses after it up to: among the Muslims; then he would say: You are the King; there is no god but You. Glory be to You; You are my Lord and I am Your servant. I have wronged myself and confessed my sin, so forgive me all my sins; none forgives sins but You. Guide me to the best manners; none guides to the best of them but You; turn its evil away from me; none turns its evil from me but You. At Your service and blessedness; I am by You and to You; no refuge nor escape from You but to You. Blessed and exalted are You; I seek Your forgiveness and repent to You. Ibrahim said: Ibn al-Munkadir narrated to me from Ali ibn Abi Talib the like of it."
"2479","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that when he began the prayer, he would say: Allah is Most Great with much greatness; much praise to Allah, good and blessed therein; then he would say: My Lord, Lord of the heavens and the earth: We shall never call upon any god besides Him; we would then have uttered an enormity. Allah is Most Great, Allah is Most Great. I have turned my face to the One who originated the heavens and the earth, and I am of the Muslims; then he would say: My Lord, Lord of the heavens and the earth: We shall never call upon any god besides Him; we would then have uttered an enormity. Allah is Most Great; praise belongs to Allah; there is no god but Allah; glory be to Allah; blessed is Allah; exalted is Allah; what Allah wills; there is no power nor strength but with Allah. I bear witness that Allah is over all things Powerful, and that Allah, glory be to Him, glory be to my Lord, the Most High; glory be to the King, the Holy, the Mighty, the Wise. My Lord, forgive me; my Lord, have mercy on me. My Lord, I seek refuge with You from the evil suggestions of the devils, and I seek refuge with You, my Lord, lest they attend me. I seek refuge with Allah from the accursed Satan; indeed Allah is the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing. He said: He used to say: This is the voluntary prayer."
"2480","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Is there any saying when a man says the takbir before he recites? He said: It has reached us that he declares Allah's oneness; when a man begins, let him magnify Allah, praise Him, remember Him, and ask if he has a need, before the recitation. He said: No named saying has reached me except like that. He said: I looked for a comprehensive saying I had seen from those before me and I said it: I say the takbir five times. He said: The first takbir with your hands, raising with your mouth. He said: So I say the takbir five times, praise five times, glorify five times, praise five times, declare Allah's oneness five times, then I say: There is no power nor strength but with Allah, five times; and I say at the end of each one of the takbir, tasbih, tahmid and tahlil: There is no power nor strength but with Allah, to the number of Your creation, the pleasure of Yourself, and the weight of Your Throne; and I ask my need, then I ask, seek forgiveness and seek refuge. He said: When I reach it I feel that in myself, I say this saying. He said: Often I fall short of that. He said: It is more beloved to me that it be in the prescribed and the voluntary prayers. I said to him: It is disliked for a man to seek forgiveness standing in the prescribed prayer; he says: Rather he glorifies and remembers Allah. He said: I have not yet recited nor prayed; this is only before the recitation. I said: If you were supplicating against a man at that time, would you name him? He said: No; I only stand for my need; as for other than that, no. A man said to him: Would you mind if I spoke at that time after the takbir and before the recitation? He said: Yes indeed, by my life; after I have said the takbir? There is no speech then, after the takbir and before the recitation."
"2481","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if I do not add to a single takbir in the prescribed prayer, and I do not say this saying: has it lapsed or is my prayer deficient? He said: No. Then he said: If you had a need from a man, would you not praise him before asking?"
"2482","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if I say: I have turned my face to the One who originated the heavens and the earth, up to: among the Muslims? He said: That is something the people innovated. Ata said: Among those who would be wakeful when he prayed tahajjud, one of them would begin and say the takbir, then remember Allah, then ask, then recite, then bow two rak'ahs, then stand and pray or begin his prayer."
"2483","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from al-Hasan, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rose at night, he would say the takbir three times, glorify three times, declare Allah's oneness three times, then say: O Allah, I seek refuge with You from Satan, from his madness, his poetry and his pride. They said: How often you seek refuge from this! He said: As for his hamz, it is madness; as for his nafth, it is poetry; as for his nafk h, it is pride."
"2484","From Ma'mar, from one who heard al-Hasan say: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rose at night, he would say: Allah is Most Great with much greatness, twice; then he would say: Allah is Most Great with much greatness; then he would say: There is no god but Allah, three times; then he would say: O Allah, I seek refuge with You from Satan, from his nafth, his nafk h and his hamz."
"2485","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Seeking refuge is obligatory for every recitation in prayer or otherwise. I said to him: Because of: When you recite the Quran, seek refuge with Allah from the accursed Satan? He said: Yes. I said: So I say: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate; I seek refuge with Allah, the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate, from the accursed Satan; and I seek refuge with You, my Lord, lest they attend me or enter my house which shelters me? He said: Before I reach much of this saying, often I leave most of it. He said: It suffices you not to add to: I seek refuge with Allah from the accursed Satan."
"2486","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if verses come to me and I recite them to you, should I seek refuge? He said: No, if you wish; but if you present the Quran, seeking in prayer or otherwise a presentation of recitation that you recite, then seek refuge for it. I said: What if I pray two light rak'ahs, should I seek refuge for them? He said: Yes."
"2487","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if I entered before the prayer and began, sought refuge, and recited until the prayer was established; should I also seek refuge for the prescribed prayer? Then I leave the prescribed prayer, then pray after it; should I also seek refuge? He said: The first seeking of refuge suffices you; if you seek refuge for that, it is good."
"2488","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I asked Nafi', the freedman of Ibn Umar, whether he knows how Ibn Umar used to seek refuge? He said: He used to say: O Allah, I seek refuge with You from the accursed Satan."
"2489","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that he used to say: My Lord, I seek refuge with You from the evil suggestions of the devils, and I seek refuge with You, my Lord, lest they attend me. I seek refuge with Allah from the accursed Satan; indeed Allah is the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing."
"2490","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, who said: Abu Dharr stood praying, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: O Abu Dharr, seek refuge with Allah from the devils of men and jinn."
"2491","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from al-Hasan, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: O Allah, I seek refuge with You from Satan, from his hamz, his nafth and his nafk h. They said: How often you seek refuge from this, for whom is this? He said: As for his hamz, it is madness; as for his nafk h, it is pride; as for his nafth, it is poetry."
"2492","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, who said: His hamz is al-mu'tah meaning madness, his nafk h is pride, and his nafth is poetry."
"2493","From al-Thawri, from Sa'id al-Jurayri, he said: Yazid ibn Abdullah ibn al-Shikhkhir narrated to us, from Uthman ibn Abi al-As, who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, Satan has come between me and my recitation. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: That is the devil called Khinzab; when you sense him, seek refuge and spit to your left three times."
"2494","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What about: And say: My Lord, I seek refuge with You from the evil suggestions of the devils? He said: A saying from the Quran; it is not obligatory in prayer."
"2495","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: If I seek refuge for two rak'ahs, then another, then another, should I seek refuge for each prayer among the seven? He said: The first suffices you; if you also seek refuge, it is good. I said: I prayed, and while I was praying a man came to me for a need, so I turned to him and he fulfilled his need, then I stood praying another time. He said: The first suffices you; if you also seek refuge, it is good."
"2496","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Seeking refuge suffices you in everything; if you add, there is no harm."
"2497","From al-Thawri, from al-Ala ibn al-Musayyab, from Ibrahim, he said: The seeking of refuge in the first thing suffices you."
"2498","From Hisham, from al-Hasan: That he used to seek refuge once at the beginning of his prayer."
"2499","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father: That he used to seek refuge before reciting the Mother of the Quran."
"2500","From Ja'far, from Ali ibn Ali al-Rifa'i, from Abu al-Mutawakkil, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri: That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say before the recitation: I seek refuge with Allah from the accursed Satan."
"2501","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin: That he used to seek refuge from Satan in prayer before reciting the Mother of the Quran, and after reciting the Mother of the Quran. He said: Al-Hasan used to seek refuge before it."
"2502","From Hisham ibn Hassan, he said: Al-Hasan used to seek refuge once when he began his prayer before reciting the Fatihah of the Book: I seek refuge with Allah, the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing, from the accursed Satan. He said: Ibn Sirin used to seek refuge in every prayer."
"2503","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I said to him: I finished the saying before the recitation. He said: Then seek refuge and recite: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate; I seek refuge with Allah, the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate, from the accursed Satan; I seek refuge with You lest they attend me and enter my house which shelters me."
"2504","From Ali, from Hammad, from Ibrahim: That he used to seek refuge after the Fatihah of the Book. Hammad said: Sa'id ibn Jubayr used to seek refuge before it."
"2505","From a man of the people of Kufa, from Abu Ishaq, he said: The companions of Ibn Mas'ud used to seek refuge after the Fatihah of the Book."
"2506","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: I forgot the seeking of refuge. He said: I do not repeat nor prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness; I shall seek refuge when I remember in my prayer. I said: We were commanded with seeking refuge as we were commanded with ablution? He said: That is not like ablution; it is words I shall say when I remember in my prayer. I said: I did not remember until I finished. He said: Good; when you finish, seek refuge."
"2507","From Ma'mar, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: Four things the imam conceals: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate; the seeking of refuge; Amin; and when he says: Allah hears him who praises Him, he says: Our Lord, to You belongs praise."
"2508","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: Five are concealed: Glory be to You, O Allah, and with Your praise; the seeking of refuge; In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate; Amin; and: O Allah, our Lord, to You belongs praise."
"2509","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, and Humayd, and Aban, from Anas: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Abu Bakr, and Umar, and Uthman reciting: Praise belongs to Allah, Lord of the worlds."
"2510","From al-Thawri, from Anas, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Abu Bakr, and Umar used to begin with: Praise belongs to Allah, Lord of the worlds. He said: I said: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate? He said: He says it behind it; he says behind it: Conceal it."
"2511","From Ma'mar, from Sa'id al-Jurayri, he said: One who heard Ibn Abdullah ibn Mughaffal informed me, saying: I recited: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate. My father said to me: Beware of innovation, O my son; I prayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Umar, and Uthman, and they used to recite: Praise belongs to Allah, Lord of the worlds."
"2512","From Isra'il, from Thuwayr ibn Abi Fakhitah, from his father: That Ali would not recite aloud: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate; he would recite aloud: Praise belongs to Allah, Lord of the worlds."
"2513","From Uthman ibn Matar, from Husayn al-Mu'allim, from Budayl al-Uqayli, from Abu al-Jawza, from A'ishah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to begin his prayer with the takbir, and begin his recitation with: Praise belongs to Allah, Lord of the worlds."
"2514","From Ma'mar, he said: One who prayed behind Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz informed me, and I heard him begin the recitation with: Praise belongs to Allah, Lord of the worlds."
"2515","Ma'mar said: Al-Hasan and Qatadah used to begin with: Praise belongs to Allah, Lord of the worlds."
"2516","From al-Thawri, from Abu Sufyan Tarif, from al-Hasan, who said: I asked him about: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate, should I recite it aloud? He said: The sunnah is: Praise belongs to Allah, Lord of the worlds; and if it is opinion, then: Praise belongs to Allah, is better than: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate."
"2517","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bashir, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, who said: Reciting aloud: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate, is the recitation of the Bedouins."
"2518","From al-Thawri, from al-Ala ibn al-Musayyab, from a man, from Ibrahim, he said: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate in the first thing suffices you, and the seeking of refuge in the first thing."
"2519","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Matar, from Abd al-Karim Abu Umayyah: That Ubayy ibn Ka'b used to begin with: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate."
"2520","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi' informed me: That Ibn Umar would never leave: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate; he would begin the recitation with: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate."
"2521","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sa'id ibn Jubayr informed him, he said: And We have given you seven of the oft-repeated, the Mother of the Quran; and I recited it to Sa'id as I recited it to you. Then he said: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate is the seventh verse. Ibn Abbas said: Allah has brought it forth for you; He did not bring it forth for anyone before you. Abd al-Razzaq said: Ibn Jurayj recited it to us: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate, a verse; Praise belongs to Allah, Lord of the worlds, a verse; the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate, a verse; Master of the Day of Judgment, a verse; You alone we worship and You alone we ask for help, a verse; Guide us along the Straight Path, a verse; the path of those You have blessed, to its end."
"2522","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Amr ibn Dinar: That Ibn Abbas used to begin the prayer with: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate."
"2523","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Salih, the freedman of al-Taw'amah, that he heard Abu Hurayrah say: It is begun with: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate, in prayer."
"2524","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, that he said: He used to begin with: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate; and he says: It is a verse of the Book of Allah, the Exalted, which the people abandoned."
"2525","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Tawus informed me that his father: When he recited to them: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate before the Mother of the Quran, he would not recite it after it."
"2526","From al-Thawri, from Asim ibn Abi al-Najud, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr: That he used to recite aloud: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate, in every rak'ah."
"2527","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: I never leave: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate, in a prescribed or voluntary prayer except forgetfully, for the Mother of the Quran and for the surah I recite after it. He said: It is a verse of the Quran. I said: It has reached me that it was not revealed with the Quran, and that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not write it until: Indeed it is from Sulayman, and indeed it is: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate, was revealed, and he wrote it then. He said: That has not reached me; it is nothing but a verse of the Quran. He said: Yahya ibn Ja'dah said: Satan has stolen from the imams the verse: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate."
"2528","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: He said to Ata: If I forget it in the prescribed prayer, do I return to the prayer or prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness? He said: Yes indeed, by my life, we drop much of the Quran. A man said to him: And Bara'ah? He said: Yes; it and al-Anfal are one; and I do not leave reciting it: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate."
"2529","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that Sa'id ibn Jubayr informed him: That the believers in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not know the end of a surah until: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate, was revealed; when: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate, was revealed, they knew that a surah had been revealed and the other had ended."
"2530","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abdullah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Hafs ibn Umar ibn Sa'd, that Mu'awiyah led the people in Madinah in the Isha prayer, and he did not recite: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate, nor some of this takbir which the people say; when he finished, those of the Emigrants and Helpers who heard that called to him and said: O Mu'awiyah, did you steal the prayer or forget? Where is: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate? And: Allah is Most Great, until you go down prostrating? Mu'awiyah never did that again afterwards."
"2531","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, from Mujahid, he said: The people have forgotten: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate, and this takbir."
"2532","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Amr ibn Dinar: That Ibn Abbas and Ibn Umar used to begin with: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate. Abu Bakr said: Ma'mar led us in prayer and began: Praise belongs to Allah, Lord of the worlds."
"2533","From Ma'mar, he said: I heard Ayyub ask Asim ibn Abi al-Najud: What did you hear about the recitation of: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate? He said: Abu Wa'il informed me that he heard Umar ibn al-Khattab begin: Praise belongs to Allah, Lord of the worlds."
"2534","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Is the recitation of the Mother of the Quran obligatory? He said: As for me, I do not leave it in the prescribed or the voluntary prayer, the opening of the Quran. He said: As for me, I heard Abu Hurayrah say: When one of you recites the Mother of the Quran, if he stops at it, it suffices him; if he adds to it, it is better."
"2535","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Mahmud ibn al-Rabi', from Ubadah ibn al-Samit, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no prayer for one who does not recite the Mother of the Quran and more."
"2536","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Hurmuz al-A'raj, that he heard Abu Sa'id al-Khudri recite the Mother of the Quran in every rak'ah, or he said: in every prayer."
"2537","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi' informed me: That Ibn Umar would never leave reciting the Mother of the Quran in every rak'ah of the prescribed prayer."
"2538","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Abu al-Aliyah, who said: I heard Ibn Umar say: I am shy before the Lord of this small house to pray a prayer in which I do not recite the Mother of the Quran and something with it. He said: I asked Ibn Abbas, and he said: Recite of it what is little or much; nothing of the Quran is little."
"2539","From Bishr ibn Rafi', he said: Dir' ibn Abdullah informed me, from Abu Umayyah al-Asadi, who said: Ubadah ibn al-Samit said to me: Recite the Mother of the Quran in every rak'ah."
"2540","From Isra'il, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Ayzar ibn Hurayth, who said: I heard Ibn Abbas say: Do not pray a prayer until you recite the Fatihah of the Book in every rak'ah."
"2541","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Does: Indeed We have given you al-Kawthar, suffice me in every rak'ah, without the Mother of the Quran with it, in the prescribed prayer? He said: No, nor the surah al-Baqarah. He said: And We have given you seven of the oft-repeated. He said: It is the seven. I said: Where is the seventh? He said: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate; and he makes the Mother of the Quran obligatory in every rak'ah."
"2542","From Ma'mar, he said: One who asked al-Hasan about a man who recited Quran in all his prayer but did not recite the Mother of the Quran, or he said: the Fatihah of the Book, informed me. He said: He does not repeat; he has recited Quran."
"2543","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if I begin with the surah Maryam, and I recite the Mother of the Quran, then I come to the prostration and prostrate, and I stand reciting the Mother of the Quran also? He said: No; you are still in the rak'ah, so do not recite in it if you wish."
"2544","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Not of those who earned anger nor of those who went astray, he would say: Amin, until those next to him could hear."
"2545","Ma'mar informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abd al-Jabbar ibn Wa'il, from his father, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Not of those who earned anger nor of those who went astray, he would say: Amin. Ma'mar said: He says amin even if he prays alone."
"2546","From Dawud ibn Qays, from Mansur ibn Maysarah, who said: I prayed with Abu Hurayrah, and when he said: Not of those who earned anger nor of those who went astray, he would say: Amin, until he made us hear, and those behind him would say amin. He said: He would say this takbir with us when he bowed and when he prostrated."
"2547","From Ma'mar and al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim: That he used to conceal amin."
"2548","From al-Thawri, from Asim, from Abu Uthman, who said: Bilal said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Do not precede me with amin."
"2549","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah: That he was a mu'adhdhin for al-Ala ibn al-Hadrami in Bahrain, and he stipulated upon him that he would not precede him with amin."
"2550","From Bishr ibn Rafi', from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Hurayrah: That he was a mu'adhdhin for al-Ala ibn al-Hadrami, and Abu Hurayrah said to him: You shall wait for me with amin, or I shall not call the adhan for you."
"2551","From Bishr ibn Rafi', from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, that Abu Hurayrah entered the mosque while the imam was leading, and Abu Hurayrah called to him: Do not precede me with amin."
"2552","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I said to him: Did Ibn al-Zubayr say amin after the Mother of the Quran? He said: Yes, and those behind him would say amin until the mosque would resound. Then he said: Amin is only a supplication. Abu Hurayrah would enter the mosque while the imam had stood before him, and say: Do not precede me with amin."
"2553","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was informed from Nafi' that Ibn Umar, when he finished the Mother of the Quran, would say: Amin; he would never leave saying amin when he finished it, and he would urge them to say it. He said: I heard from him a report about that."
"2554","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Tawus said to me: He only knows his father saying it, the imam and those behind him."
"2555","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Amin? He said: I never leave it. He said: After the Mother of the Quran in the prescribed and the voluntary prayer? He said: I used to hear the imams saying after the Mother of the Quran: Amin, they themselves and those behind them, until the mosque would resound."
"2556","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyab, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the imam says: Not of those who earned anger nor of those who went astray, then say: Amin; for the angels say: Amin, and the imam says: Amin; whoever's saying of amin coincides with the angels' saying of amin, his past sins are forgiven. From Ma'mar, from Hammam ibn Munabbih, that he heard Abu Hurayrah say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, and he mentioned the like of al-Zuhri's hadith."
"2557","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Ata say: I heard Abu Hurayrah say: When amin on earth coincides with amin in heaven, his past sins are forgiven."
"2558","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from Yunus ibn Jubayr, from Hattan ibn Abdullah al-Riqashi, from Abu Musa al-Ash'ari, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the imam says: Not of those who earned anger nor of those who went astray, then say: Amin; Allah will answer you."
"2559","From Ma'mar, he said: One who heard Ikrimah say informed me: The rows of the people of earth are upon the rows of the people of heaven; when amin on earth coincides with amin in heaven, he is forgiven."
"2560","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: The Jews did not envy you for anything as they envied you for amin, and for the greeting of peace with which some of you greet others. He said: That reached me from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2561","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, he said: Amin is a name among the names of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic."
"2562","From Bishr ibn Rafi', from Abu Abdullah, from Abu Hurayrah, who says: When Musa ibn Imran would supplicate, Harun would say amin to his supplication."
"2563","He said: I heard Abu Hurayrah say: Amin is a name among the names of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic."
"2564","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said to me: I marvel at a man who supplicates and makes his supplication a continuous recitation, not saying amin to his supplication. He said: He says: Amin."
"2565","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: When the imam recites the Mother of the Quran in the last rak'ah of Maghrib and the last two of Isha, how does he say amin? He said: He whispers amin to himself."
"2566","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: I forgot amin. He said: Do not repeat, nor prostrate for forgetfulness."
"2567","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What is recited aloud of the recitation of the night and day prescribed prayers? He said: Fajr, the first two of Isha, the first two of Maghrib, and Jumu'ah when it is in congregation; as for when a man is alone, then no; it is like Zuhr then, and Fitr then. He said: I think Adha is like Fitr."
"2568","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi', who said: Meaning Ali, he would recite in the first two of Zuhr and Asr the Mother of the Quran and a surah, and would not recite in the last two."
"2569","Al-Zuhri said: Jabir ibn Abdullah used to recite in the first two rak'ahs of Zuhr and Asr the Mother of the Quran and a surah, and in the last two the Mother of the Quran. Al-Zuhri said: The people follow their imam."
"2570","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, who said: He would not recite in the last two, and he would call them the two glorifications."
"2571","From Abu Hanifah, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: Alqamah never recited a letter in the last two rak'ahs."
"2572","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: Recite in the first two the Fatihah of the Book and a surah, and in the last two glorify."
"2573","From al-Thawri, from Ibrahim, he said: He would not recite in the last two."
"2574","Hammad said: Sa'id ibn Jubayr used to recite the Fatihah of the Book."
"2575","From Dawud ibn Qays, from Ubaydullah ibn Miqsam, who said: I asked Jabir ibn Abdullah. He said: As for me, I recite in the first two rak'ahs of Zuhr and Asr the Fatihah of the Book and a surah, and in the last two the Fatihah of the Book. From al-Thawri, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Ubaydullah ibn Miqsam, from Jabir ibn Abdullah the like of it."
"2576","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Aziz, from Dhakwan: That A'ishah used to recite in the last two the Fatihah of the Book."
"2577","From Ma'mar ibn Rashid, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ya'ish ibn al-Walid, from Khalid ibn Ma'dan, that Abu al-Darda used to say: I recite in the first two rak'ahs of Zuhr, Asr and the last Isha, in every rak'ah the Mother of the Quran and a surah, and in the last rak'ah of Maghrib the Mother of the Quran."
"2578","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Does the Mother of the Quran in the prescribed prayer in all four suffice me? He said: Yes. I said: Do we add in Zuhr and Asr to the Mother of the Quran? He said: Yes; Say: He is Allah, the One, and the like of that. I said: Do we add in the last of Maghrib and the last two of Isha to the Mother of the Quran? He said: Yes; Say: He is Allah, the One, and the like of that."
"2579","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if I do not recite in the prescribed prayer in the separated portion, and I recite part of the surah from its beginning, middle or end? He said: It does not harm you; it is all Quran."
"2580","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Abbad ibn Ja'far say: Abu Salamah ibn Sufyan, Abdullah ibn Amr ibn Abd al-Qari, and Abdullah ibn al-Musayyab al-Abidi informed me, from Abdullah ibn al-Sa'ib, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed Fajr in Makkah and began the surah al-Mu'minun until, when he came to the mention of Musa and Harun, or Isa - Ibn Abbad doubts, or they differed upon him - the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was seized by a cough, so he cut short and bowed; and Abdullah ibn al-Sa'ib was present at that."
"2581","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid: That Ibn Mas'ud led them in Isha and recited forty verses of al-Anfal, then recited in the second a surah of the Mufassal."
"2582","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, who said: Ibn Mas'ud led us in the last Isha prayer, and began with the surah al-Anfal until, when he reached: An excellent Protector and an excellent Helper, he bowed; then he recited in the second a surah of the Mufassal."
"2583","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Nafi': Did Ibn Umar recite in a rak'ah of the prescribed prayer part of a long surah then bow? He said: No."
"2584","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: They used to recite in Zuhr and Asr, in the first two rak'ahs the Fatihah of the Book and what was easy, and in the last two the Fatihah of the Book."
"2585","From al-Thawri, from Ali ibn Zayd ibn Jad'an, from al-Hasan and others, who said: Umar wrote to Abu Musa: Recite in Maghrib the short Mufassal, in Isha the middle Mufassal, and in Fajr the long Mufassal."
"2586","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, he said: The first of the prayers is longer in recitation."
"2587","From Isra'il, he said: Isa ibn Abi Azzah informed me that he heard al-Sha'bi say: The first of the prayers is longer in recitation."
"2588","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abdullah ibn Abi Qatadah, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to lead us in Zuhr and would sometimes let us hear a verse; he would lengthen the first rak'ah of the Fajr prayer, and lengthen the first rak'ah of the Zuhr prayer; we thought he meant by that that the people should catch the first rak'ah."
"2589","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Umarah ibn Umayr, from Abu Ma'mar, who said: We asked Khabbab: Did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recite in Zuhr and Asr? He said: Yes. We said: By what did you know that? He said: By the movement of his beard."
"2590","From al-Thawri, from Zayd al-Ammi, from Abu al-Aliyah, who said: The companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) observed him in Zuhr and estimated his recitation in the first rak'ah of Zuhr as the revelation of al-Sajdah."
"2591","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Mijlaz: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prostrated in the Zuhr prayer, then stood and recited; they think he recited Alif Lam Mim, the revelation, while praying with his companions."
"2592","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from Murriq al-Ijli, who said: Ibn Umar would pray and recite in Zuhr Qaf and: The Hour drew near. Ma'mar said: A sheikh of ours informed me, from Murriq al-Ijli. We said: From where did you know? He said: I would sometimes hear a verse from him. From Ja'far ibn Sulayman, from Aban, from Murriq al-Ijli the like of Qatadah's hadith."
"2593","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar: That he used to recite in Zuhr: Those who disbelieve, and: Indeed We have opened for you. From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar the like of it."
"2594","From al-Thawri, from Husayn ibn Abd al-Rahman, who said: I asked Ibrahim: How much do you recite in the first rak'ah? He said: About thirty verses."
"2595","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Abi Najih, and from Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from his father, from Malik ibn Aws ibn al-Hadathan, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: I liken the day prayer to the night prayer, the midday prayer."
"2596","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah: That Ibn Umar used to recite in the first rak'ah of Zuhr: By the scattering winds."
"2597","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Asr used to be made lighter than Zuhr in recitation."
"2598","From Ma'mar, from Thabit: That Anas used to lead us in Zuhr and Asr, and would sometimes let us hear of his recitation: When the sky splits, and: Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High."
"2599","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: He recites in the first two rak'ahs of the Asr prayer: When the sky bursts asunder, and: By the sky with towers."
"2600","From Ja'far ibn Sulayman, from Aban, from Murriq, who said: We prayed Asr with Ibn Umar, and he recited: The emissaries, and: About what they question one another."
"2601","From al-Thawri, from Ziyad ibn al-Fayyad, who said: Tamim ibn Salamah asked Ibrahim, while I was listening, about the recitation in Asr. He said: It is like Maghrib. Sufyan said: The measure of the recitation of Asr is: By the night when it covers, and: Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High, and: By the fig and the olive."
"2602","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Abi Mulaykah say: Urwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me that Marwan ibn al-Hakam informed him. He said: Zayd ibn Thabit said to me: Why do you recite in Maghrib the short Mufassal? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite in the Maghrib prayer the longer of the two long ones. He said: I said: What is the longer of the two long ones? He said: Al-A'raf. He said: I said to Ibn Abi Mulaykah: What are the two long ones? He said: As if he said from his own opinion: Al-An'am and al-A'raf."
"2603","Ma'mar informed me, from al-Zuhri, from Muhammad ibn Jubayr ibn Mut'im, from his father, who had come regarding the ransom of the captives, the captives of the day of Badr. He said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recite in Maghrib: By the Mount."
"2604","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Uthman ibn Abi Sulayman informed me that Jubayr ibn Mut'im said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited in Maghrib: By the Mount."
"2605","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah, from Ibn Abbas, from his mother Umm al-Fadl, who said: The last I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recite in Maghrib was the surah al-Mursalat."
"2606","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from a man who heard Ibn Umar: Recite in Maghrib: Qaf, by the Glorious Quran."
"2607","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, who said: Salih ibn Kaysan informed me that he heard Ibn Umar recite in Maghrib: Indeed We have opened for you a clear opening."
"2608","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Maymun, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab led us in the Maghrib prayer, and recited in the first rak'ah: By the fig and the olive, and the Mount of Sinai, and in the last rak'ah: Have you not seen, and: For the protection, together."
"2609","From Malik, from Abu Ubayd, the freedman of Sulayman ibn Abd al-Malik, that Ubadah ibn Nusayy informed him that he heard al-Qays ibn al-Harith say: Abu Abdullah al-Sunabihi informed me: That he prayed behind Abu Bakr al-Siddiq the Maghrib, and he recited in the first two rak'ahs the Mother of the Quran and two surahs of the short Mufassal; then he stood in the third rak'ah. He said: I drew near him until my clothes almost touched his clothes, and I heard him recite the Mother of the Quran and this verse: Our Lord, do not swerve our hearts after You have guided us, up to: the Bestower. Abu Ubayd said: Ubadah informed me that he was with Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz in his caliphate, and Umar said to Qays: How did you inform me from Abu Abdullah? He narrated to him, and Umar said: We have not left it since we heard it, though before that I was upon other than that. A man said: Upon what was the Commander of the Believers before that? He said: I used to recite: Say: He is Allah, the One."
"2610","From Isma'il ibn Abdullah Abu al-Walid, from Ibn Awn, from Raja ibn Haywah, from Mahmud ibn Rabi', that al-Sunabihi said: I prayed behind Abu Bakr the Maghrib where my clothes touch his clothes; when he was in the last rak'ah, he recited the Fatihah of the Book, then recited: Our Lord, do not swerve our hearts after, up to: the Bestower. Abu Bakr said: Muhammad ibn Rashid informed me. He said: I heard a man narrate it to Makhul, from Sahl ibn Sa'd al-Sa'idi, that he heard Abu Bakr recite it in the third rak'ah; Makhul said to him: It was not a recitation from Abu Bakr; it was only a supplication from him."
"2611","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: Whoever prayed Maghrib and recited within himself but made himself hear it, it suffices him."
"2612","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from Abdur Rahman ibn Yazid, he said: Ibn Mas'ud led us in the last Isha prayer, and he opened with Surah al-Anfal, until when he reached 'Excellent is the Protector and excellent is the Helper' he bowed, then in the second rak'ah he recited a surah from the Mufassal. From ath-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Abdur Rahman ibn Yazid, from Ibn Mas'ud, the like of it."
"2613","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Ibn Abi Mulaykah saying: Alqamah ibn Abi Waqqas informed me, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab used to recite Surah Yusuf in the last Isha. He said: and I was at the back of the row, until when he mentioned Yusuf I heard his weeping, and I was at the back of the rows."
"2614","From Ibn Jurayj, Ibrahim ibn Maysarah said from Ibn Tawus: that his father would never omit reciting in the last Isha the lesser Surah of Prostration, Alif Lam Mim Tanzil, and 'Blessed is He in Whose Hand is the Dominion.'"
"2615","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, from Salamah ibn Wahram, he said: I saw Tawus countless times reciting in the last Isha Alif Lam Mim Tanzil al-Sajdah and Tabarak, and prostrating in it; then one night he did not prostrate in it, so I thought he bowed when he reached the prostration; he recited it over two rak'ahs."
"2616","From Abdullah ibn Kathir, from Shu'bah, he said: Adi ibn Thabit informed me that he heard al-Bara ibn Azib saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited in the Isha prayer, in one of the two rak'ahs, 'By the fig and the olive,' while on a journey."
"2617","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Abbad ibn Ja'far saying: Abu Salamah ibn Sufyan, Abdullah ibn Umar, Ibn Abd al-Qari, and Abdullah ibn al-Musayyab informed me, from Abdullah ibn al-Sa'ib, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us in Fajr at Makkah, and he opened Surah al-Mu'minun until when he came to the mention of Musa and Harun — or the mention of Isa, Ibn Abbad being uncertain, or they differed against him — the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was seized by a cough, so he bowed. And Abdullah ibn al-Sa'ib was present at that."
"2618","From ath-Thawri, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from Abu Abdur Rahman as-Sulami, he said: Ali led us in Fajr and recited al-Anbiya, then he left out a verse, then recited further on, then returned to the verse and recited it, then resumed anew and went back to what he had been reciting."
"2619","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Hafsah bint Sirin: that Umar recited Surah Yusuf in Fajr and faltered, so he went back to its beginning, then recited and went on with his recitation."
"2620","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Safiyyah bint Abi Ubayd: that Umar recited in the Fajr prayer al-Kahf, and Yusuf or Yusuf, and Hud. He said: and he faltered in Yusuf, so when he faltered he returned to the beginning of the surah and recited, then went through all of it."
"2621","Ma'mar informed us, from az-Zuhri, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: I prayed Fajr behind Abu Bakr, and he opened al-Baqarah and recited it in two rak'ahs. Then Umar stood when he finished and said: May Allah forgive you, the sun had almost risen before you gave salam. He said: Had it risen, it would have found us not heedless."
"2622","Ma'mar informed us, from Qatadah, from Anas: I prayed behind Abu Bakr and he opened Surah Al Imran. Then Umar stood up to him and said: May Allah forgive you, the sun had almost risen before you gave salam. He said: Had it risen, it would have found us not heedless."
"2623","From Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father: that Abu Bakr recited al-Baqarah in the two rak'ahs of Fajr."
"2624","From ath-Thawri, from Amr ibn Ya'la, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr: that he led them in Fajr and recited Bani Isra'il in two rak'ahs."
"2625","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabi'ah, he said: I memorized Surah Yusuf and Surah al-Hajj only from Umar, because of how often he used to recite them in the Fajr prayer. He said: he used to recite them with slow recitation."
"2626","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Isma'il ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'd, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Shaddad saying: I heard the sobbing of Umar, while I was in the row behind him in prayer, as he recited Surah Yusuf, until he reached 'I complain of my anguish and sorrow only to Allah.'"
"2627","From Ma'mar, from Asim ibn Sulayman, from Abu Uthman an-Nahdi, he said: Umar led us in the morning prayer, and he did not finish until every sensible person knew the sun had risen. He said: so it was said to him: You did not finish until the sun had almost risen. He said: Had it risen, it would have found us not heedless."
"2628","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sulayman ibn Atiq informed me that Umar ibn al-Khattab recited Surah Al Imran in the morning prayer."
"2629","From ath-Thawri, from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, from his uncle Qutbah ibn Malik, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reciting in the first rak'ah of the Fajr prayer, 'And tall date-palms with arranged clusters.'"
"2630","From Isra'il, from Simak ibn Harb, that he heard Jabir ibn Samurah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray the prayer like your prayer that you pray today, but he used to lighten it; his prayer was lighter than yours; he used to recite in Fajr al-Waqi'ah and similar surahs."
"2631","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from al-Walid ibn Sari', from Amr ibn Hurayth, he said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reciting in the morning prayer, 'By the night when it departs.'"
"2632","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Ibn al-Munkadir, he said: Rabi'ah ibn Abdullah ibn al-Hudayr narrated to us, he said: Umar used to recite al-Hadid and the like."
"2633","From Malik, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar: that he used to recite in Fajr ten from the beginning of the Mufassal, one surah in each rak'ah."
"2634","From ath-Thawri and Ibn Uyaynah, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim at-Taymi, from Husayn ibn Sabrah: that Umar recited Yusuf in Fajr, then in the second he recited an-Najm, then prostrated, then stood, then recited 'When the earth is shaken.'"
"2635","From ath-Thawri, from Abdul Malik ibn Umayr, from Shabib Abu Rawh, from a man among the Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed the Fajr prayer and recited Surah ar-Rum, and got confused in part of it. When he finished, he said: What is the matter with people who pray with us without purification? Whoever prays with us, let him perfect his ablution, for those ones only confuse the Qur'an for us."
"2636","From ath-Thawri and Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: Adi ibn Artah ordered al-Hasan to lead the people in prayer, and he recited in Fajr 'O Prophet, when you divorce women' and 'O Prophet, why do you forbid.'"
"2637","From ath-Thawri, from al-Ala ibn al-Musayyab: that Abu Wa'il recited in one of the two rak'ahs of the morning prayer the Mother of the Qur'an and a verse."
"2638","From Muhammad, from Muslim al-Batin, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite on Friday in Fajr 'Tanzil as-Sajdah' and 'Has there come upon man.' From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, the like of it, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2639","From Ma'mar, from Abdul Malik ibn Umayr: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited in Fajr on Friday Surah ar-Rum."
"2640","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abu Farwah al-Hamdani, he said: I heard Abu al-Ahwas saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite in the Fajr prayer on Friday 'Tanzil as-Sajdah' and 'Has there come upon man.'"
"2641","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Barzah: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited in the morning prayer 'Indeed, We have granted you a clear victory.'"
"2642","From Abdullah ibn Kathir, from Shu'bah, from al-Hajjaj, from al-Hakam, he said: I heard Amr ibn Maymun saying: I prayed with Umar at Dhu al-Hulayfah when he was heading for Makkah the Fajr prayer, and he recited 'Say: O disbelievers' and 'The One, the Eternal,' in the recitation of Ibn Mas'ud."
"2643","From Ma'mar, from al-A'mash, from al-Ma'rur ibn Suwayd, he said: I was with Umar between Makkah and Madinah, and he led us in Fajr and recited 'Have you not seen how your Lord dealt' and 'For the accustomed security of Quraysh.' Then he saw people alighting and praying in a mosque, so he asked about them, and they said: A mosque in which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed. He said: Those before you were destroyed only because they took the traces of their prophets as churches. Whoever passes by any mosque and the prayer comes, let him pray; otherwise let him go on."
"2644","From ath-Thawri, from Malik ibn Mighwal, from al-Hakam, from Amr ibn Maymun, he said: I accompanied Umar ibn al-Khattab on a journey, and he recited 'Say: O disbelievers' and 'Say: He is Allah, the One.'"
"2645","From ath-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq at-Taymi, from Amr ibn Maymun, he said: I prayed with Umar in the year he was killed, at Makkah, the morning prayer, and he recited 'I swear by this city' and 'By the fig and the olive.'"
"2646","From Ma'mar, from Abdur Razzaq, from his father: that he used to recite in the morning prayer while traveling 'Glorify' and 'Has there reached you the story of the Overwhelming' and the like."
"2647","From ath-Thawri and Ibn Uyaynah, from as-Salt ibn Bahram: that Ibrahim an-Nakha'i led them while traveling, and he recited in the morning prayer 'When the earth is shaken' and 'Indeed, We sent it down in the Night of Decree.'"
"2648","Ma'mar informed us, from Thabit al-Bunani, he said: I was with Anas ibn Malik, and he was returning from his land heading for Basrah, and between it and Basrah were three miles or three farsakhs, when the morning prayer came. So a son of his called Abu Bakr stood and led us in prayer, and he recited Surah Tabarak. When he gave salam, Anas said to him: You made it long for us."
"2649","From ath-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Maymun, he said: I prayed the morning prayer on the day Umar was killed, and nothing kept me from standing with the first row except awe of Umar. He said: then the people surged, and they put forward Abdur Rahman ibn Awf, and he recited 'When the help of Allah comes and the victory' and 'Indeed, We have granted you al-Kawthar.'"
"2650","Al-Fadl informed us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: They used to recite in the Fajr prayer while traveling 'When the sky splits open' and 'Has there reached you the story of the Overwhelming.'"
"2651","From Ibn Uyaynah, from as-Salt ibn Bahram: that Ibrahim an-Nakha'i led them while traveling in the morning prayer, and he recited 'By the morning brightness' and 'By the fig.'"
"2652","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I heard Abu Hurayrah saying: In every prayer there is recitation; what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made audible to us, we make audible to you, and what he concealed from us, we conceal from you. And I heard him saying: There is no prayer except with recitation."
"2653","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Al-Ala ibn Abdur Rahman ibn Ya'qub informed me that Abu as-Sa'ib, the freedman of Banu Abdullah ibn Hisham ibn Zuhrah, informed him that he heard Abu Hurayrah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever prayed a prayer and did not recite in it the Mother of the Qur'an, it is deficient, it is deficient, it is deficient, incomplete."
"2654","From Malik, from Wahb ibn Kaysan, he said: I heard Jabir ibn Abdullah saying: Whoever prayed a rak'ah and did not recite in it the Mother of the Qur'an has not prayed, except with the imam."
"2655","From ath-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Ata, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to lead us, reciting aloud and softly; so we recite aloud in what he recited aloud, and softly in what he kept soft. And I heard him saying: There is no prayer except with recitation."
"2656","From ath-Thawri, from Abu Khalid, from Ibrahim, from Abdullah ibn Abi Awfa, he said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I cannot learn the Qur'an, so what suffices me? He said: Say: Glory be to Allah, and praise be to Allah, and there is no power nor strength except with Allah, and there is no god but Allah, and Allah is Greatest. He said: so the man said: Like this, and he gathered his five fingers. He said: This is for Allah. He said: Say: O Allah, forgive me, have mercy on me, guide me, and provide for me. He said: so the man clasped both his palms together, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: As for this one, he has filled his hands with good. Sufyan said: And that was the counting of the Arabs."
"2657","From Abdullah ibn Umar, he said: Muhammad ibn Ibrahim at-Taymi narrated to us, from Abu Salamah ibn Abdur Rahman, that Umar ibn al-Khattab prayed a prayer and did not recite in it, so he was told of that, and he said: Did I complete the bowing and prostration? They said: Yes. He said: Then he did not repeat that prayer."
"2658","From Isra'il, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, that a man came to him and said: I prayed and did not recite. He said: Did you complete the bowing and prostration? He said: Yes. He said: Is your prayer complete? He said: Yes. He said: Not everyone is good at recitation."
"2659","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: The Muslim man must have six surahs which he learns for prayer: two surahs for the morning prayer, two surahs for Maghrib, and two surahs for prayer in Isha."
"2660","From Ikrimah ibn Ammar, he said: Damdam ibn Jaws al-Hiffani narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Hanzalah, he said: He narrated to us while sitting with Abu Hurayrah, he said: I prayed Maghrib behind Umar ibn al-Khattab, and he did not recite anything in the first rak'ah, then he recited in the second the Mother of the Qur'an twice and two surahs, then he prostrated two prostrations before the salam."
"2661","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ikrimah ibn Khalid informed me that Umar ibn al-Khattab prayed the last Isha at al-Jabiyah and did not recite in it until he finished. When he finished, he went in, and Abdur Rahman ibn Awf circled around him and cleared his throat for him until Umar ibn al-Khattab heard his sound and knew he had a need. He said: Who is this? He said: Abdur Rahman ibn Awf. He said: Do you have a need? He said: Yes. He said: Then come in. So he came in and said: Tell me, what you did just now — did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) enjoin it upon you, or did you see him doing it? He said: And what is it? He said: You did not recite in Isha. He said: Did I do that? He said: Yes. He said: I forgot; I dispatched a caravan from Syria until it reached Madinah. He said: Who is the mu'adhdhin? So he called the iqamah for prayer, then he returned and prayed Isha for the people. When he finished, he addressed them, he said: There is no prayer for one who did not recite in it. What I did just now was forgetfulness; I dispatched a caravan from Syria until it reached Madinah, and I distributed it. I said: From whom do you narrate this? He said: I do not know, except that I took it only from a trustworthy one."
"2662","From Isra'il ibn Yunus, from Jabir ibn al-Ju'fi, he said: Ziyad ibn Iyad al-Ash'ari narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab led us in Isha and I did not hear his recitation in it. So Abu Musa al-Ash'ari said to him: Why did you not recite, O Commander of the Believers? He said: Is that so, O Abdur Rahman ibn Awf? He said: Yes. He said: So he ordered the mu'adhdhin, and he called the iqamah for prayer, and he recited a recitation, and I heard it while I was at the back of the rows. When he finished, he said: I was praying while talking to myself about a caravan I had sent from Madinah with its saddles and blankets — when will it come? And there is no prayer except with recitation."
"2663","From ath-Thawri, from Jabir, and Ibn Awn, from ash-Sha'bi: that Umar prayed Maghrib and did not recite, so he ordered the mu'adhdhin, and he repeated the adhan and the iqamah, then he repeated the prayer."
"2664","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: Umar led the people in the Isha prayer, and I did not hear his recitation in it. So Abu Musa al-Ash'ari said to him: Why did you not recite, O Commander of the Believers? He said: Is that so, O Abdur Rahman ibn Awf? He said: Yes. He said: Did I do that? They said: Yes. He said: You spoke the truth. He said: I dispatched a caravan from Madinah until it reached Syria, and I was moving it on stage by stage. He said: So he repeated the prayer for them. He said: Then Aban informed me, from Jabir ibn Yazid, that Umar ibn al-Khattab ordered the mu'adhdhin, and he called the iqamah, then he prayed."
"2665","From Isra'il, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, he said: If a man forgets to recite in the first two rak'ahs of Zuhr and Asr and Isha, let him recite in the last two rak'ahs, and it suffices him."
"2666","From Ma'mar, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: I asked Alqamah, he said: I said: I forgot in the first two rak'ahs, then I recited in the last two rak'ahs — does it suffice for my prayer? He said: Yes, if Allah wills."
"2667","From ath-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: I asked Alqamah about a man who forgot to recite in the first two and recited in the last two? He said: It suffices him, if Allah wills. Sufyan said: And we say: let him prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness."
"2668","From ath-Thawri, he said: It reached me from Ibrahim, he said: If he did not recite in three of Zuhr, he repeats."
"2669","From Ma'mar, from Hammad, from Abdul Haqq, from Ibrahim, that he said: If a man forgets the recitation in Zuhr and Asr, he repeats; and if he recited in two rak'ahs and did not recite in two rak'ahs, he does not repeat; and if he recited in one rak'ah and did not recite in three of Zuhr, he repeats."
"2670","From Ma'mar, from one who heard al-Hasan saying: About a man who forgot to recite in one rak'ah and did not recite in the other, he said: He repeats the rak'ah in which he did not recite. Ma'mar said: Repeating is more pleasing to me."
"2671","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: If I forgot the recitation in a rak'ah — the Mother of the Qur'an and the surah after it — I did not recite anything in the rak'ah? He said: Then do not repeat, but prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness."
"2672","From ath-Thawri, he said: If he did not recite in the two rak'ahs of Maghrib, he repeats."
"2673","From ath-Thawri, he said: If he did not recite in a rak'ah until he bowed, then he raises his head when he remembers and recites, then he prostrates the two prostrations of forgetfulness; but if he prostrated, he goes on."
"2674","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to his Companions: Do you recite behind me while I am reciting? He said: So they were silent until he asked them three times. They said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Then do not do that; let each one of you recite the Opening of the Book within himself, silently."
"2675","From ath-Thawri, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abu Qilabah, from Muhammad ibn Abi A'ishah, from a man among the Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Perhaps you recite while the imam is reciting — two or three times. They said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah, we do so. He said: Then do not do so, except that one of you recite the Opening of the Book."
"2676","From Ibn Jurayj, from al-Ala ibn Abdur Rahman, that Abu as-Sa'ib, the freedman of Banu Abdullah ibn Hisham ibn Zuhrah, informed him that he heard Abu Hurayrah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever prayed a prayer and did not recite in it the Mother of the Qur'an, it is deficient, it is deficient, incomplete."
"2677","Abu as-Sa'ib said: I am sometimes behind the imam. Abu as-Sa'ib said: So Abu Hurayrah nudged my arm and said: O Bedouin, recite it within yourself, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Allah said: I have divided the prayer between Me and My servant into two halves; half of it is for Me and half is for My servant, and My servant shall have what he asks. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Recite. The servant stands and says: Praise be to Allah, Lord of the worlds. Then Allah says: My servant has praised Me. And the servant says: The Most Merciful, the Most Merciful. Then Allah says: My servant has extolled Me. And the servant says: Master of the Day of Judgment. Then Allah says: My servant has glorified Me. And He said: This is between Me and My servant. Then the servant says: You alone we worship and You alone we ask for help. Then Allah says: Its reward is for My servant, and he shall have what he asks. My servant says: Guide us along the Straight Path, to the end of the surah. Allah says: This is for My servant, and he shall have what he asks."
"2678","From Malik, from al-Ala ibn Abdur Rahman ibn Ya'qub, that he heard Abu as-Sa'ib, the freedman of Banu Zuhrah, narrating that he heard Abu Hurayrah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever prayed a prayer and did not recite in it the Mother of the Qur'an, it is deficient, it is deficient, incomplete."
"2679","He said: So I said to him: O Abu Hurayrah, I am sometimes behind the imam. He said: So he nudged my arm, then said: Recite it, O Persian, within yourself, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: I have divided the prayer between Me and My servant into two halves; half of it is for Me and half is for My servant, and My servant shall have what he asks. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Recite. The servant stands and says: Praise be to Allah, Lord of the worlds. Allah, Blessed and Exalted, says: My servant has praised Me. And the servant says: The Most Merciful, the Most Merciful. Allah says: My servant has extolled Me. And the servant says: Master of the Day of Judgment. Allah says: My servant has glorified Me. He said: And this verse is between Me and My servant, and My servant shall have what he asks. The servant says: You alone we worship and You alone we ask for help, to the end of the surah. Then these are for My servant, and My servant shall have what he asks."
"2680","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, from Makhul: He used to recite the Opening of the Book in what the imam recites aloud and in what he does not recite aloud."
"2681","From Bishr ibn Rafi', he said: Dar' ibn Abdullah informed me, from Abu Umayyah al-Azdi, he said: Ubadah ibn as-Samit said to me: Recite the Mother of the Qur'an in every prayer — or he said: in every rak'ah. He said: I said: Do you recite it, O Abu al-Walid, with the imam? He said: I do not leave it, as imam nor as follower."
"2682","From Ja'far ibn Sulayman, from Ibn Awn, he said: Raja ibn Haywah narrated to us, he said: I prayed beside Ubadah ibn as-Samit, and I heard him reciting behind the imam. When we finished our prayer, we said: O Abu al-Walid, do you recite with the imam? He said: Woe to you, there is no prayer except with it."
"2683","From Yahya ibn al-Ala, from Ibn Sinan, from Abdullah ibn Abi al-Hudhayl: that Ubayy ibn Ka'b used to recite behind the imam in Zuhr and Asr."
"2684","From at-Taymi, from Layth, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, he said: He must recite the Opening of the Book behind the imam, whether he recites aloud or not."
"2685","From ath-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Mujahid, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Amr reciting behind the imam in Zuhr and Asr."
"2686","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Husayn, from Abdur Rahman, he said: I heard Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah reciting in Zuhr and Asr with the imam, so I asked Ibrahim, and he said: Do not recite unless the imam errs. And I asked Mujahid, and he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Amr reciting."
"2687","From ath-Thawri, from Sulayman ash-Shaybani, from Jawwab, from Yazid ibn Sharik, that he said to Umar: Should I recite behind the imam? He said: Yes. I said: Even if you recited, O Commander of the Believers? He said: Yes, even if I recited."
"2688","From Ibn at-Taymi, from Layth, from Ash'ath, from Abu Yazid, from al-Harith ibn Suwayd and Yazid at-Taymi, they said: Umar ibn al-Khattab ordered us to recite behind the imam."
"2689","From ath-Thawri, from as-Salt ar-Raba'i, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: If the imam does not make you hear, then recite."
"2690","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If you do not understand the imam's recitation, then recite if you wish, or glorify Allah."
"2691","From Malik, from Abu an-Nadr, from Malik ibn Abi Amir, that Uthman said: For the silent one who does not hear is a share like that of the listening, attentive one. And Ibn Jurayj mentioned it, from Mus'ab, from Muhammad ibn Uthman, except that he said: Of reward."
"2692","From Abdur Rahman ibn Zayd ibn Aslam, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: For the silent one who does not hear is like the reward of the silent one who hears."
"2693","From Ma'mar, from az-Zuhri, he said: The imam recites the Opening of the Book and another surah in Zuhr and Asr in the first two rak'ahs."
"2694","From ath-Thawri, from az-Zuhri, he said: If the imam recites aloud, do not recite anything. From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, the like of it."
"2695","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: As for me, I recite with the imam in Zuhr and Asr the Mother of the Qur'an and a short surah, then I declare Allah's oneness and glorify Him. I said: Do I make the one beside me hear my recitation? He said: With the imam? He said: I said: Yes. He said: No."
"2696","From Ibn al-Muthanna ibn as-Sabbah, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from Abdullah ibn Amr, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed the people and said: Whoever prayed an obligatory or a supererogatory prayer, let him recite the Mother of the Qur'an and Qur'an with it; but if he ends at the Mother of the Qur'an, it suffices him. And whoever is with the imam, let him recite before him or when he is silent. Whoever prayed a prayer and did not recite in it, it is deficient — three times."
"2697","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If the imam recites aloud, let him hasten with the Mother of the Qur'an, or let him recite after he becomes silent; then when he recites, let them listen attentively, as Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said."
"2698","From Ma'mar and Ibn Jurayj, they said: Ibn Khuthaym informed us, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, that he said: He must recite the Mother of the Qur'an with the imam; but those who passed, when the imam said the takbir, would be silent a while, not reciting, as much as they would recite the Mother of the Qur'an."
"2699","From Ma'mar, from one who heard al-Hasan saying: Recite the Mother of the Qur'an, whether the imam recites aloud or not; but when he recites aloud and finishes the Mother of the Qur'an, then recite it yourself."
"2700","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Sharik ibn Abi Namir, from Urwah ibn az-Zubayr, he said: When the imam says 'Not of those who earned anger nor of those who went astray,' I recited the Mother of the Qur'an, or after he finishes the surah after it."
"2701","From Ma'mar, from more than one, from al-Hasan, he said: Samurah ibn Jundab used to lead the people, and he would pause two pauses: when he said the takbir for prayer, and when he finished reciting the Mother of the Qur'an. So the people blamed him, and he wrote to Ubayy ibn Ka'b about that, that the people blamed me: I forgot and they remembered, or I remembered and they forgot. So Ubayy wrote to him: Rather, you remembered and they forgot. And al-Hasan used to say: When the imam finishes reciting the Mother of the Qur'an, then recite it yourself."
"2702","From al-Muthanna, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from Abdullah ibn Amr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When the imam says 'Not of those who earned anger nor of those who went astray,' I recited the Mother of the Qur'an, or after he finishes."
"2703","From Ma'mar, or another, from Ibn Khuthaym, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: The Mother of the Qur'an is a must; but those who passed, when the imam said the takbir, would be silent a while, not reciting, as much as they recite the Mother of the Qur'an."
"2704","From Ma'mar, from az-Zuhri, he said: I heard Ibn Ukaymah narrating from Abu Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed a prayer in which he recited aloud, then he turned to the people after he gave salam and said to them: Did any one of you recite with me just now? They said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: I say: Why am I being contended with over the Qur'an? So the people stopped reciting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what he recites aloud of the recitation when they heard that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab informed me, he said: I heard Ibn Ukaymah narrating from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it, up to his saying: Why am I being contended with over the Qur'an?"
"2705","From ath-Thawri, from Musa ibn Abi A'ishah, from Abdullah ibn Shaddad ibn al-Had al-Laythi, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed Zuhr or Asr, and a man began reciting behind the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while a man forbade him. When he prayed, he said: O Messenger of Allah, I was reciting and this one was forbidding me. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Whoever has an imam, the recitation of the imam is recitation for him."
"2706","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from a man, from Imran ibn al-Husayn, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed Zuhr with his Companions. He said: When he finished, he said: Did any one of you recite 'Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High'? A man said: I recited it. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I said: Why am I being contended with over it?"
"2707","Ma'mar informed us, from Qatadah, from Zurarah ibn Abi Awfa, from Imran ibn al-Husayn, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed Zuhr with his Companions. When he completed his prayer, he said: Which of you recited 'Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High'? Some of the people said: I did, O Messenger of Allah. He said: I knew that some of you were contending with me over it."
"2708","From ath-Thawri, from Musa ibn Abi A'ishah, from al-Walid ibn Abi Bashir, he said: A man recited 'Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High' behind the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and that was mentioned to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said: It has been mentioned that some of you were contending with me over it."
"2709","From al-Hasan ibn Umarah, from Abdur Rahman ibn al-Asbahani, from Abdullah ibn Abi Layla, he said: I heard Ali saying: Whoever recited behind the imam has missed the natural way."
"2710","From Dawud ibn Qays, he said: Umar ibn Muhammad ibn Zayd ibn Umar ibn al-Khattab informed me, he said: Musa ibn Sa'id narrated to me, from Zayd ibn Thabit, he said: Whoever recited with the imam, he has no prayer."
"2711","From Mansur, from Abu Wa'il, he said: A man came to Abdullah and said: O Abu Abdur Rahman, should I recite behind the imam? He said: Listen attentively to the Qur'an, for in prayer there is occupation, and the imam will suffice you that."
"2712","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abu Ishaq ash-Shaybani, from a man, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab enjoined that you not recite with the imam."
"2713","Ibn Uyaynah said: So our companions informed us, from Zubayd, from Abdullah ibn Abi Layla, from Ali, he said: Reciting with the imam is not of the natural way."
"2714","From ath-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Layla, from a man, from Abdullah ibn Abi Layla, the brother of Abdur Rahman ibn Abi Layla: that Ali used to forbid reciting behind the imam."
"2715","From Dawud ibn Qays, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, he said: Ali said: Whoever recited with the imam is not upon the natural way. He said: And Wala said: And Ibn Mas'ud said: May his mouth be filled with dust."
"2716","He said: And Umar ibn al-Khattab said: I wish that the one who recites behind the imam had a stone in his mouth."
"2717","From ath-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, he said: I wish that the one who recites behind the imam had his mouth filled with dust."
"2718","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, that Alqamah ibn Qays said: I wish that the one who recites behind the imam had his mouth filled — he said: I think he said: with dust or with hot stones."
"2719","From Ma'mar, he said: And a man informed me, from al-Aswad, that he said: I wish that the one who recites behind the imam, when he recites aloud, would bite upon a live coal."
"2720","From Abdur Rahman ibn Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade reciting behind the imam. He said: Our elders informed me that Ali said: Whoever recited behind the imam, he has no prayer."
"2721","He said: And Musa ibn Uqbah informed me: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Abu Bakr, and Umar, and Uthman used to forbid reciting behind the imam."
"2722","From Ma'mar and Ibn Jurayj, from az-Zuhri, from Salim ibn Abdullah, he said: The recitation of the imam suffices you in what he recites aloud in prayer."
"2723","Ibn Jurayj said: And Ibn Shihab narrated to me, from Salim, that Ibn Umar used to say: One listens attentively to the imam in what he recites aloud in prayer, and does not recite with him."
"2724","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Anas ibn Sirin, he said: I asked Ibn Umar: Should I recite with the imam? He said: You are indeed big-bellied. The recitation of the imam."
"2725","From Isra'il, from Abu Ishaq, he said: The companions of Abdullah would not recite behind the imam."
"2726","Dawud ibn Qays informed us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ibn Umar: He used to forbid reciting behind the imam."
"2727","From ath-Thawri, from Ibn Dhakwan, from Zayd ibn Thabit and Ibn Umar: They would not recite behind the imam."
"2728","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: The recitation of the imam suffices for those behind him. I said: From whom do you report it? He said: I heard it, but I prefer that you take the merits; I prefer that you recite with him."
"2729","From Yahya ibn al-Ala, he said: Al-A'mash informed us, from Ibrahim, he said: They would not recite behind the imam until the time of Ibn Ziyad; then it was said to them: When he does not recite aloud, he does not recite within himself, so the people recited."
"2730","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Does his recitation suffice for those behind the imam, in what he raises the voice in and in what he keeps soft? He said: Yes."
"2731","From Dawud ibn Qays, from Ubaydullah ibn Miqsam, he said: I asked Jabir ibn Abdullah: Do you recite anything behind the imam in Zuhr and Asr? He said: No."
"2732","From Ibn Jurayj, from more than one, from al-Hasan, he said: Murrah used to lead the people, pausing two pauses: when he said the takbir for prayer, and when he finished reciting the Qur'an. The people blamed that in him. He wrote to Ubayy ibn Ka'b about that, that the people blamed me; perhaps I forgot and they remembered, or I remembered and they forgot. So Ubayy wrote to him: Rather, you remembered and they forgot. And al-Hasan used to say: When the imam finishes reciting the Qur'an, then recite it yourself."
"2733","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, that Ali said: No people should prompt the imam while he is reciting, for it is speech."
"2734","From Isra'il, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Do not ever prompt an imam while you are in prayer."
"2735","From Isra'il, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Ibn Mas'ud, he said: If the imam falters, do not repeat to him, for it is speech."
"2736","From ath-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, he said: They used to dislike prompting the imam."
"2737","He said: And al-Mughirah said, from Ibrahim: If you hesitate in a verse, pass it by to another."
"2738","From Isra'il, from Abu Ishaq, from Abidah ibn Rabi'ah, he said: I came to the mosque, and there was a man praying behind the Maqam, sweet-smelling, well-dressed, and he was reciting, with a man beside him prompting him. So I said: Who is this? They said: Uthman."
"2739","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi' informed me, he said: I used to prompt Ibn Umar in prayer, and he would say nothing."
"2740","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi': that Ibn Umar prayed Maghrib, and when he recited 'Not of those who earned anger nor of those who went astray,' he began reciting 'In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Merciful' repeatedly, and he repeated it. So I said: When the earth is shaken — so he recited it. When he finished, he did not blame me for that."
"2741","From Ma'mar, from az-Zuhri, he said: Marwan ibn al-Hakam used to be prompted in that time, and he had appointed men for that, so that when he erred they prompted him, while the Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were that day in Madinah. Ma'mar said: And I heard Qatadah saying: Do not prompt him until he falls silent; when he falls silent, then prompt him."
"2742","From Ma'mar, he said: One who heard al-Hasan informed me, he saying: Prompt your brother."
"2743","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I asked Ata: Is there any harm in prompting the imam? He said: No, and what is it but Qur'an?"
"2744","From ath-Thawri, from Abdul A'la, from Abu Abdur Rahman as-Sulami, he said: When they ask you to feed them, feed them. He says: When he falters, repeat to him."
"2745","From Ma'mar, from az-Zuhri, from Ibrahim ibn Abdullah ibn Hunayn, from his father, from Ali, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade me from reciting in bowing and prostration, and from wearing a gold ring, and from wearing qassi, and from wearing saffron-dyed cloth. I said to him: What is qassi? He said: Silk."
"2746","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Hunayn, from Ali, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade me to recite while I am bowing."
"2747","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu Ja'far, he said: Ali ibn Abi Talib said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade me — and I do not say: he forbade you — from reciting while I am bowing."
"2748","From ath-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, he said: Do not recite while you are bowing, nor while you are prostrating."
"2749","From al-Hasan ibn Umarah, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Ali, I love for you what I love for myself, and I dislike for you what I dislike for myself. Do not wear qassi, nor saffron-dyed cloth, nor ride upon red saddle-cloths, for they are the mounts of Satan. And do not recite while you are prostrating, and do not gather up your hair while you pray, for it is the share of Satan. And do not recite while you are bowing, and do not recite while you are prostrating, and do not prompt the imam of a people, and do not play with pebbles in prayer."
"2750","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata al-Khurasani informed me: that Ibn Abbas used to dislike reciting when a man was bowing or prostrating."
"2751","From ath-Thawri, from Uthman ibn al-Aswad, from Mujahid, he said: Do not recite in bowing nor in prostration; bowing and prostration were made only for glorification."
"2752","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Sulayman ibn Suhaym, from Abdullah ibn Ma'bad, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) lifted the curtain and saw the people in rows behind Abu Bakr, and said: Nothing remains of the glad tidings of prophethood except the righteous dream which the Muslim sees or which is seen for him. And I have been forbidden to recite in bowing and prostration. As for bowing, the Lord is magnified in it; and as for prostration, strive in supplication in it, for it is likely to be answered for you. He says: That is, it is worthy."
"2753","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What do you think, if I raised my head in prostration in the obligatory prayer, and I rose reciting before I stand upright? He said: I do not like that you recite until you stand upright."
"2754","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: that he heard Ubayd ibn Umayr reciting while bowing and prostrating in supererogatory prayer. Ata said: And I do not dislike reciting while bowing and prostrating in supererogatory prayer; but as for the obligatory, I dislike it; rather, glorify Allah and declare His oneness."
"2755","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim narrated to me, from Sa'id — and his father was a slave boy of Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman — and he informed him, from Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman: that he passed by the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) one night while he was praying in the mosque in Madinah. He said: So I stood praying behind him, imagining he did not know. He opened Surah al-Baqarah, so I said: When he comes to a hundred verses, he will bow. He came to it and did not bow. So I said: When he comes to two hundred verses, he will bow. He came to it and did not bow. When he finishes it, he will bow. He finished it and did not bow. When he finished, he said: O Allah, to You is praise, O Allah, to You is praise — singly. Then he opened Al Imran, so I said: If he finishes it, he will bow. He finished it and did not bow, and said: O Allah, to You is praise — three times. Then he opened Surah al-Ma'idah, so I said: When he finishes, he will bow. He finished it and bowed. I heard him saying: Glory be to my Lord, the Most Great, while moving his lips, so I know he was saying other than that. Then he prostrated, and I heard him saying: Glory be to my Lord, the Most High, while moving his lips, so I know he was saying other than that, but I understand nothing but it. Then he opened Surah al-An'am, so I left him and went away."
"2756","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim informed me, from a man, he said: One of the household of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed me that he spent the night with him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rose at night, relieved himself, then came to the water-skin and poured out water, washed his hands three times, then rinsed his mouth and performed ablution, and recited the seven long surahs in a single rakah."
"2757","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yazid ibn Khusayfah, from al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid: Uthman recited the seven long surahs in one rakah."
"2758","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi informed me: Ibn Umar used to recite three surahs in one rakah on some occasions."
"2759","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi: Ibn Umar used to recite two or three surahs in one rakah."
"2760","From Dawud ibn Qays, he said: I heard Raja ibn Haywah asking Nafi: Did Ibn Umar combine two surahs in one rakah? He said: Yes, and more surahs."
"2761","From Ibn Abi Rawwad, from Nafi: Ibn Umar used to recite [several] surahs in one rakah."
"2762","From al-Thawri and Abu Hanifah, from Hammad, from Said ibn Jubayr, he said: I heard him reciting the Quran inside the Kabah in one rakah, and in the other rakah he recited Say: He is Allah, the One."
"2763","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, from Ibn Tawus, he said: My father used to combine Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High and By the night when it covers in one rakah, and By the morning brightness and Did We not expand in one rakah in the obligatory prayer."
"2764","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: He saw no harm in combining surahs in one rakah. Ibn Jurayj said: Tawus used to combine three surahs in one rakah."
"2765","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, from Yazid ibn Zadawayh: Tawus used to recite Say: He is Allah, the One along with the Mother of the Quran in every rakah."
"2766","From al-Thawri, from Asim, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Ibn Umar: He used to recite ten surahs in one rakah."
"2767","From Hushaym, from Yala ibn Ata, from Ibn Nafi ibn Lubaybah, he said: I said to Ibn Umar, or someone else said: I recited al-Mufassal in one rakah. He said: Did you do that? If Allah had willed He would have sent it down as one whole. Give every surah its share of bowing and prostration."
"2768","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Umarah ibn Umayr, from Abu Mamar, from Abu Masud al-Ansari, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The prayer in which a man does not straighten his back in bowing and prostration does not suffice."
"2769","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What about people who place their hands below their knees when they bow? He said: This is an innovation; no, except above the knees."
"2770","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: A man said to Ata: I see people, when they bow, lowering their heads until they place their chins between their legs. He said: No, this is an innovation; those who passed before did not do that. He said: How then? He said: A moderate bowing, like the bowing of people nowadays."
"2771","From Ibn Mujahid, from his father, from Ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to a man: When you stand for prayer and bow, place your hands on your knees and spread your fingers, then raise your head until every limb returns to its joint; and when you prostrate, press your forehead firmly on the ground and do not peck."
"2772","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from al-Qasim ibn Abi Bazzah, from a man, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said to a man: When you bow, place your hands on your knees and spread your fingers."
"2773","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Najih, he said: I said: Did Mujahid use to say: When he places his hands [on his knees] he has completed [the bowing]? He nodded his head, meaning yes."
"2774","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Salih, freedman of al-Tawamah, that he heard Abu Hurayrah saying: There is no prayer except with bowing."
"2775","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abu Husayn, he said: I saw a very old shaykh wearing a hooded cloak - Ibn Uyaynah said, meaning al-Aswad ibn Yazid - when he bowed he clasped his hands between his knees. He said: So we came to Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami and told him, and he said: Yes, those are the companions of Abdullah ibn Masud, but Umar has established for you [placing hands on] the knees, so hold to the knees."
"2776","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abu Yafur, from Musab ibn Sad, he said: I prayed beside my father and joined my hands [between my knees]. My father forbade me and said: We used to do it, then we were forbidden from it."
"2777","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: Abdullah used to do tatbiq; when he bowed he placed his hands between his knees and spread his forearms and thighs. I said to Ibrahim: What prevents you from that? He said: And he used to place his hands on his knees."
"2778","From Israil, from Abu Ishaq, from Alqamah and al-Aswad, they said: We prayed with Abdullah, and when he bowed he joined his palms and placed them between his knees, and he struck our hands, so we did that. Then we later met Umar, and he led us in prayer in his house; when he bowed we joined our palms as Abdullah had joined, while Umar placed his hands on his knees. When he finished, he said: What is this? We told him of Abdullahs practice. He said: That was something done, then abandoned."
"2779","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said to me: Fix your hands on your knees and fix your back, and that suffices for completing the bowing."
"2780","From Mamar and al-Zuhri, he said: Settle in bowing until everything in you settles in its place."
"2781","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Uthman ibn Abi Sulayman informed me, from Ibn Masadah, the commander of the armies, he said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: I have grown heavy in body, so whoever misses the bowing will catch me in the slowness of my standing."
"2782","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: A man said to Ata: It was said that a person should not lower his head in bowing nor raise it up? He said: No, and why would he lower it? A man said to him: What is iqna? He said: Raising his head in bowing."
"2783","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: It was disliked to raise the head up or lower it down in bowing."
"2784","From al-Thawri, from Abu Farwah al-Juhani, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his bowing, his prostration, and his standing after the rakah were close to equal. He said: And if a cup of water had been placed on the back of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), it would not have spilled, due to his levelness when bowing."
"2785","From Uthman ibn Matar, from Husayn the teacher, from Budayl al-Uqayli, from Abu al-Jawza, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he bowed, neither lowered his head nor raised it up; and when he raised his head from bowing, he did not prostrate until he stood straight."
"2786","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim narrated to me, from Said - whose father was a servant boy of Hudhayfah - that he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying while bowing: Glory be to my Lord, the Tremendous, and his lips moved, so I knew he was saying something else besides that."
"2787","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Silah ibn Zufar, from Hudhayfah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he bowed, said: Glory be to my Lord, the Tremendous, and when he prostrated, said: Glory be to my Lord, the Most High."
"2788","From al-Thawri and Qatadah, that Ali said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he bowed, used to say: O Allah, I bowed, in You I believed, You are my Lord, and upon You I relied; and in prostration: Glory be to my Lord, the Most High."
"2789","From al-Thawri, from Mamar, from Asim, from Abu al-Nujud, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, he said: Ali said: Among the most beloved speech to Allah, mighty and majestic, is for the servant to say: My Lord, I have wronged myself, so forgive me."
"2790","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Abu al-Duha, from Masruq, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say often in his bowing and prostration: Glory be to You, O Allah, our Lord, and with Your praise; O Allah, forgive me - interpreting the Quran, meaning When the help of Allah comes and the victory."
"2791","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Ubaydah, from Abdullah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say often, when When the help of Allah comes and the victory was revealed: Glory be to You, O Allah, and with Your praise; O Allah, forgive me; You are the Oft-Returning."
"2792","From Bishr ibn Rafi, from Yahya ibn Rafi, from Abu Ubaydah ibn Abdullah, that Ibn Masud, when he bowed, said: Glory be to my Lord, the Tremendous, three times and more; and when he prostrated, said: Glory be to my Lord, the Most High, and with His praise, three times and more. Abu Ubaydah said: And my father used to mention that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say it."
"2793","From Mamar, from Imran, that Aishah rose one night seeking the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the depth of the night. He said: Her hand fell upon the sole of the foot of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was prostrating, saying: Glory be to my Lord, Possessor of dominion, compulsion, grandeur and greatness; I seek refuge in Allah, by Your pleasure from Your anger, by Your forgiveness from Your punishment, and by You from You; I cannot count praise of You; You are as You praised Yourself. From Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from Aishah, the like of it."
"2794","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yahya ibn Said, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Taymi, he said: Aishah missed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) one night, so she reached with her hand and it fell upon the sole of his foot while he was prostrating, saying: I seek refuge by Your pardon from Your punishment, by Your pleasure from Your anger, and by You from You; I cannot attain Your praise nor count praise of You; You are as You praised Yourself."
"2795","He said: Mamar informed us, from Qatadah, from Mutarrif ibn Abdullah, from Aishah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say in his bowing and prostration: Most Glorious, Most Holy, Lord of the angels and the Spirit."
"2796","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, that Umar ibn al-Khattab used to say in his bowing and prostration, for the length of five glorifications: Glory be to Allah and with His praise."
"2797","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata al-Khurasani informed me, that Ibn Abbas said: Bow until your palms rest firmly on your knees for the length of three glorifications, then raise your back until every limb of yours takes its place."
"2798","From Mamar, from one who heard al-Hasan saying: Sufficient in bowing and prostration is Glory be to Allah and with His praise, three times."
"2799","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: He used to say when he prostrated: My face has prostrated to the One who created it, fashioned it, and made its hearing and sight."
"2800","He said: Mamar informed us, from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud, he said: I heard Shaqiq ibn Salamah, while prostrating, saying: O Allah, forgive me."
"2801","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Misar, from Said ibn Abi Burdah, from his father, he said: I prayed beside Ibn Umar and heard him saying while prostrating: My Lord, shield me from Your punishment on the Day You resurrect Your servants."
"2802","From al-Thawri, from Ali ibn al-Aqmar, from Abu al-Aswad and Shaddad ibn al-Azma, from Ibn Masud, he said: We differed; Abu al-Aswad said: Abdullah used to say in his prostration: Glory be to You, there is no Lord but You; and Shaddad said: He used to say: Glory be to You, there is no god but You."
"2803","He said: Mamar informed us, from Qatadah, from Umm al-Hasan: She heard Umm Salamah saying in her prostration and in her prayer: O Allah, forgive, have mercy, and guide us to the straightest path. And Abdullah ibn Kathir mentioned it, from Shubah, from Qatadah, from Umm al-Hasan, from Umm Salamah."
"2804","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Misar, from Said ibn Abi Burdah, from his father, he said: I prayed beside Ibn Umar and heard him saying: My Lord, for what You bestowed upon me, I shall never be a supporter of the criminals. When he finished his prayer, he said to me: I never prayed a prayer but I hoped it would be an expiation for what came before it."
"2805","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Jafar ibn Muhammad, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to al-Hattabah when they asked him: Three glorifications bowing and three glorifications prostrating, for al-Hattabah - meaning a people who came to him."
"2806","From Yahya ibn al-Ala, from Shubah, from his uncle, from Abu Ishaq, from Alqamah, he said: I entered the mosque and found Abdullah praying. He bowed, so I began Surah al-Araf, and I finished before he prostrated."
"2807","From al-Thawri, from Hisham, from Ibn Sirin, he said: When he places his hands on his knees he has completed, and when he presses his forehead on the ground he has completed. Sufyan said: Even if he does nothing else."
"2808","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: al-Walid ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Mughith informed me that he heard Abu Abdullah ibn Bajilah - and he was approved, regarded and transmitting hadith - saying: A man from the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He recited Surah al-Baqarah, reciting it well, clearly and beautifully. He did not pass a verse mentioning Paradise but asked for it, nor a verse mentioning Hell but sought refuge at it, until he finished it. Then he bowed and said: Glory be to the Lord of dominion, compulsion, grandeur and greatness. Then he raised his head and said the like of that when he raised his head. Then he prostrated and remained a while, saying the like of what he had said while raising his head from the rakah. Then he raised his head, stood, and recited Al Imran in the same manner. Then he finished it and did in bowing, prostration, and raising the head from bowing and prostration as he had done in the first rakah. The man said to him in the morning: O Prophet of Allah, I wanted to pray with your prayer but I could not. He said: You cannot do what I can; I am the most fearful of Allah among you."
"2809","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Has any reported saying to be said in bowing reached you? He said: No. I said: What do you say yourself? He said: When I am not in haste and nothing distracts me, I say a saying; when I reach it, that is it. I say: Glory be to You and with Your praise, there is no god but You, three times; Glory be to our Lord, indeed the promise of our Lord is fulfilled, three times; Glory be to Allah the Tremendous, three times; Glory be to Allah and with His praise, three times; Glory be to the King, the Most Holy, three times; Most Glorious, Most Holy, Lord of the angels and the Spirit; My Lords mercy has preceded His anger, three times. I said: Has it reached you that he used to say any of them in bowing? He said: No. I said: What do you follow in that? He said: As for Glory be to You and with Your praise, there is no god but You."
"2810","Ibn Abi Mulaykah informed me, from Aishah, she said: I missed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) one night and thought he had gone to one of his wives. I felt around, then returned, and there he was, bowing and prostrating, saying: Glory be to You and with Your praise, there is no god but You. She said: I said: May my father and mother be ransom for you, I am in one matter and you are in another. He said: As for Glory be to our Lord, indeed the promise of our Lord is fulfilled, I follow with it the one in Surah Bani Israil; and as for Glory be to Allah the Tremendous and Glory be to Allah and with His praise, I magnify Allah with them; and as for Glory be to the King, the Most Holy."
"2811","It reached me, from Ubayd ibn Umayr, that he said: The Lord, blessed and exalted, descends in the last half of the night to the heaven, saying: Who asks Me that I may give him? Who seeks My forgiveness that I may forgive him? And the angel says: Glorify the King, the Most Holy, until dawn comes, then the Lord ascends. So I follow the angels saying: Glory be to the King, the Most Holy; and as for Most Glorious, Most Holy, my Lords mercy has preceded His anger."
"2812","It reached me: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he was taken by night, every time he passed a company the angels greeted him, until when he came to the sixth heaven, Jibril said to him: This is an angel, so greet him. The angel hastened and greeted him first. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I wished I had greeted him before he greeted me. When he came to the seventh heaven, Jibril said to him: Allah, mighty and majestic, prays. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Does He pray? He said: Yes. He said: What is His prayer? He said: He says: Most Glorious, Most Holy, Lord of the angels and the Spirit; My mercy has preceded My anger. So I follow that. He said: I said: May I put some of that before some? He said: If you wish."
"2813","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I say in prostration the like of what I say in bowing."
"2814","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Ibn Tawus asked about the completion of prostration, so he gestured with his hand and said: Three glorifications. Abu Bakr said: And Muhammad ibn Muslim mentioned it, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, from Tawus."
"2815","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ata, he said: I often heard Ibn al-Zubayr saying in his prostration: Most Glorious, Most Holy, Lord of the angels and the Spirit; my Lords mercy has preceded His anger."
"2816","From al-Hasan ibn Umarah, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali, he said: Ali used to say when he bowed: O Allah, to You I humbled myself, to You I bowed, to You I submitted, in You I believed, You are my Lord, upon You I relied. Humbled to You are my hearing, sight, flesh, blood, brain, bones, sinews, hair and skin. Glory be to Allah, glory be to Allah, glory be to Allah. When he said: Allah hears whoever praises Him, he said: O Allah, our Lord, to You is praise. When he prostrated, he said: O Allah, to You I prostrated, to You I submitted, in You I believed, upon You I relied, You are my Lord. Prostrated to You are my hearing, sight, flesh, blood, bones, sinews, hair and skin. Glory be to Allah, glory be to Allah, glory be to Allah."
"2817","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Abdullah ibn al-Fadl, from Abd al-Rahman al-Araj, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Rafi, from Ali, he said: The speech of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his bowing was to say: O Allah, to You I bowed, in You I believed, to You I submitted, You are my Lord; humbled are my hearing, sight, brain, bones and sinews to Allah, Lord of the worlds. When he raised his head from bowing, he said: Allah hears whoever praises Him, then followed it with: O Allah, to You is praise, filling the heavens, filling the earth, and filling whatever You will besides. When he prostrated, he said in his prostration: O Allah, to You I prostrated, in You I believed, to You I submitted, You are my Lord; my face has prostrated to the One who created it and split its hearing and sight. Blessed is Allah, the Best of creators. From Ibn Abi, from Ibn al-Munkadir, from Ali, the like of it."
"2818","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, he said: Tawus used to say in his bowing: O Allah, to You I bowed, to You I humbled myself, to You I submitted, in You I believed, upon You I relied, to You I turned, and to You is the return."
"2819","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: It is sunnah, when you raise your head from the rakah or the prostration, to stand straight until every bone of it returns to its joint. When you do that, it suffices you. And it was said - I do not know whether the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said it - after he raised his head from the rakah and stood straight, say: O Allah, our Lord, to You is praise, filling the heavens, filling the earth, and filling whatever You will besides; none withholds what You give, none gives what You withhold, and the wealth of the wealthy does not avail him against You."
"2820","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Awn ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he said: Allah hears whoever praises Him, said: O Allah, our Lord, to You is praise, filling the heavens, filling the earth, and filling whatever You will besides."
"2821","From Ibrahim ibn Umar, he said: Wahb ibn Manus informed me, he said: I heard Said ibn Jubayr narrating, from Ibn Abbas: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he raised his head from bowing, said: Allah hears whoever praises Him, then said: O Allah, our Lord, and to You is praise, filling the heavens, filling the earth, and filling whatever You will besides."
"2822","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Anas ibn Malik, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the imam says: Allah hears whoever praises Him, say: Our Lord, to You is praise. From Ibn Jurayj, the like of it with this chain."
"2823","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he raised his head from the rakah, say: Our Lord, to You is praise."
"2824","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he raised his head from bowing, said: O Allah, our Lord, and to You is praise."
"2825","From Mamar, from Qatadah, from Yunus ibn Jubayr, from Hittan ibn Abdullah al-Raqashi, from Abu Musa al-Ashari, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the imam says: Allah hears whoever praises Him, say: Our Lord, to You is praise; Allah will hear you, for Allah has decreed upon the tongue of His Prophet: Allah hears whoever praises Him."
"2826","From Ali: When he said: Allah hears whoever praises Him, he said: O Allah, our Lord, to You is abundant praise, then he prostrated. He said: O Allah, our Lord, to You is praise; O Allah, by Your power and strength I stand and sit."
"2827","From al-Thawri, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from al-Ahwas, he said: When the imam says: Allah hears whoever praises Him, whoever is behind him should say: Our Lord, to You is praise."
"2828","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ismail ibn Umayyah, from Said ibn Abi Said: He heard Abu Hurayrah, while leading people in prayer, saying: Allah hears whoever praises Him; O Allah, our Lord, to You is praise; Allah is greatest - raising his voice with that, and we followed together."
"2829","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani, he said: I heard Abd al-Rahman ibn Hurmuz al-Araj saying: I heard Abu Hurayrah saying: When the imam raises his head from bowing and says: Allah hears whoever praises Him, say: Our Lord, to You is praise."
"2830","From Ibn al-Taymi, he said: I heard Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he raised his head from bowing, said: Praise be to Allah, Possessor of dominion, compulsion, grandeur and greatness."
"2831","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Dawud ibn Sabur, from Mujahid, he said: A man said when he raised his head from the rakah: Our Lord, to You is abundant, good, blessed praise. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished his prayer, he said: Who said the words? The man fell silent. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who said them? The man said: I did, O Messenger of Allah. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Twelve angels raced for them, each writing them."
"2832","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If you are with an imam and he says: Allah hears whoever praises Him, then if you also say: Allah hears whoever praises Him, it is good; and if you do not say it with the imam, it suffices you. To combine them both with the imam is dearer to me."
"2833","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said: What if the imam does not let me hear his saying: Allah hears whoever praises Him? He said: Say the like of what he says when he lets you hear. He said: The imam praises when he says: Allah hears whoever praises Him, and a man praying alone praises; both praise while standing before prostrating. The imam and others are commanded to praise when he says: Allah hears whoever praises Him. And whoever is behind the imam says what has been recorded."
"2834","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If you say when you raise your head from the rakah: Praise be to Allah, it suffices you; whenever you praise, whatever praise, it suffices you."
"2835","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: He raises his head from prostration until everything settles in its place."
"2836","From Mamar, from Mansur, from Salim ibn Abi al-Jad, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he prostrated, spread his arms until the whiteness of his armpits could be seen."
"2837","From Dawud ibn Qays, he said: I heard Ubayd Allah ibn Abdullah ibn Aqram narrating, from his father, he said: My father narrated to me that he was with his father at al-Qa of Namirah - or he said Tamirah. He said: Riders passed us and halted at the side of the road. My father said to me: O my son, stay with our lambs until I approach these riders. He said: He approached them, and I approached with him. Prayer was established, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was among them. He said: I kept looking at the whiteness of the armpits of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) each time he prostrated."
"2838","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Tamimi, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the whiteness of his armpits could be seen when he prostrated."
"2839","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he prostrated, the whiteness of his armpits could be seen."
"2840","Ibn Uyaynah said: And Ubayd Allah ibn Abdullah informed me, from Yazid ibn al-Asamm, from Maymunah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he prostrated, spread his arms so that if a lamb wanted to pass beneath his hand, it could pass."
"2841","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: It was disliked to be excessively long in prostration or to hold back, but a middle course between that."
"2842","Ibrahim said: And I was told that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the whiteness of his armpit could be seen when he prostrated."
"2843","From al-Thawri, from Adam ibn Ali, he said: Ibn Umar saw me praying without lifting my forearms from the ground, and said: O nephew, do not spread like the beast spreads; support yourself on your palms and show your upper arms, for when you do that, every limb of yours prostrates."
"2844","From al-Thawri, from Sumayy, he said: al-Numan ibn Abi Ayyash al-Zuraqi narrated to us, he said: The Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) complained of leaning on their hands in prostration, so they were permitted to help themselves with their hands upon their knees in prostration. Sufyan said: And it is a concession for the night worshipper."
"2845","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman ibn Musa, that Jabir ibn Abdullah said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanding moderation in prostration, and that a man should not prostrate spreading his forearms like a dog."
"2846","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you prostrates, be moderate, and do not spread his forearms as a dog spreads."
"2847","From Dawud ibn Qays, from Dawud ibn Aslam, he said: The Muslims complained to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about spreading out in prayer, so they were commanded to help themselves with their knees."
"2848","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, he said: Ibn Umar, when he saw a man spreading his fingers in prayer in prostration, forbade him. He said: And he himself used to join his fingers together and spread them [flat]."
"2849","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from Hafs ibn Asim, he said: I prayed beside Ibn Umar, and I spread my fingers when I prostrated. He said: O nephew, join your fingers when you prostrate, face the qiblah, and face the qiblah with your palms, for they prostrate along with the face."
"2850","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi informed me that Ibn Umar used to say: When one of you prostrates, let him place his hands with his face, for the hands prostrate as the face prostrates; and when he raises his head, let him raise them with it."
"2851","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: When one of you prostrates, let him set down his hands, for the hands prostrate along with the face."
"2852","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibrahim ibn Maysarah informed me, from Tawus, he said: I never saw a worshipper like Abdullah ibn Umar in facing the Kabah with his face, palms and feet."
"2853","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Ajlan, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, he said: Ibn Umar liked to be straight in prayer, even his fingers toward the qiblah."
"2854","From Uthman ibn Matar, from Husayn, from Budayl al-Uqayli, from Abu al-Jawza, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to forbid us that one of us spread his forearms as a dog or beast spreads."
"2855","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I asked Ata about jutting out with the hands in prostration. He said: It is forbidden. I said: But I place my elbows? He said: If you wish, upon your knees; and if you wish, do not place them upon them. If you do not jut out, it does not harm you wherever you place them."
"2856","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: He used to forbid us that a man place his forearms on the ground up to the palms when he prostrated."
"2857","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Habib, from Abu al-Shatha, from Ibn Umar, that he saw a man turning aside when he prostrated, and said: No, do not overturn your form - meaning, do not single it out. I said: What is overturn your form? He said: Do not change; do not shrink back."
"2858","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Abu Wail, he said: Abdullah said: When one of you prostrates, let him not prostrate leaning on one side nor lying down, for when he does prostration well, all his bones prostrate."
"2859","From al-Thawri, from Amr ibn Qays, from Masruq, he said: He saw a man, when he prostrated, raise his feet to the sky, and said: The prayer of this one is not complete."
"2860","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Ajlan, from Bukayr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, from Amir ibn Sad, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded placing the palms and erecting the feet."
"2861","Sufyan said: And it reached me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to erect his feet in prostration and place the toes on the ground."
"2862","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman ibn Musa, he said: Fix in prostration your knees and the fronts of your feet on the ground."
"2863","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if I do not straighten my back in the prostration of the obligatory prayer, and do not fix my face prostrating in some of that? He said: Do not repeat, and do not prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness."
"2864","From al-Thawri, from Abu al-Hudhayl, from Ibrahim, he said: It was disliked for a man, when he prostrated, to touch the ground with his private part. He said: Its explanation is that there should be a garment between him and the ground."
"2865","From al-Thawri, from Asim ibn Kulayb, from his father, from Wail ibn Hujr, he said: I watched the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); when he prostrated, his hands were level with his ears."
"2866","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, he said: Ibn Umar used to place his hands level with his ears when he prostrated."
"2867","From Ismail ibn Ubayd Allah ibn Abi al-Walid, from Ibn Awn, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, he said: Ibn Umar was asked where a man should place his hand when he prostrates? He said: Throw them where they fall."
"2868","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Is there a prescribed place for the palms in prostration? He said: No."
"2869","From Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from Alqamah and al-Aswad: Ibn Masud bowed, joining his hands and placing them between his knees."
"2870","From Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from Musab ibn Sad, he said: I bowed and joined, placing my hands between my knees, and my father forbade me, saying: We used to do that, then we were forbidden from it."
"2871","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: Allah hears whoever praises Him, when he raised his back from the rakah, saying while standing: Our Lord, and to You is praise, then saying takbir when going down prostrating."
"2872","From al-Thawri and Mamar, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim: Umar, when he bowed, fell as a camel falls, his knees before his hands, saying takbir as he went down."
"2873","From al-Thawri, from Asim, from Ibrahim, about a man whose hands fall before his knees, Ibrahim said: None does that but a madman."
"2874","From al-Thawri, from Mamar, from Ibrahim, about a man whose hands fall before his knees, Ibrahim said: None does that but a madman."
"2875","From al-Taymi, from Kahmas, from Abdullah ibn Yasar: When he prostrated, he placed his knees, then his hands, then his face; and when he wanted to stand, he raised his face, then his hands, then his knees. Abd al-Razzaq said: How fine a hadith it is, and how I admire it."
"2876","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from Ibn al-Zubayr, he said: He would only say takbir while going down and in his rising to stand."
"2877","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me: He saw Muawiyah in the third rakah; in the third after bowing, when he raised his head from prostration he did not linger. He said: He rises saying takbir in his rising to stand. Ata said: I wondered at that until it reached me that the practice was that."
"2878","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim: He disliked leaning on his hands when sitting between the two rakahs and when rising."
"2879","From Hushaym ibn Bashir, from Mansur and Yunus, from al-Hasan: He saw no harm in a man leaning on his hands when rising in prayer."
"2880","From Mamar, from Qatadah, about a man rising to stand: Does he lift his hands first or his knees? He said: He considers whichever is easier for him."
"2881","From Abdullah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar: He used to stand, when he raised his head from prostration, leaning on his hands before lifting them."
"2882","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: I see people, when one of them stands, folding his leg - with its foot - then placing his hand on his thigh, then standing thus; or placing his hand on the ground, then standing upon it. He said: This standing is closer to pride; in prayer only humility befits."
"2883","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Abi Layla, he said: I heard Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid saying: I watched Abdullah ibn Masud in prayer and saw him rise without sitting; he rose upon the fronts of his feet in the first and third rakah."
"2884","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, he said: Abdullah used to rise upon the fronts of his feet from the last prostration, in the first and third rakah. From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Abu Atiyyah, that Ibn Abbas and Ibn Umar used to do that."
"2885","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar: He used to stand, when he raised his head from prostration, leaning on his hands before lifting them."
"2886","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was commanded to prostrate on seven, and not to gather hair nor garment: on the forehead and nose - then he passed his hands over his forehead and nose - and the palms, knees and feet."
"2887","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that he heard Tawus - thinking he traced it - from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was commanded to prostrate on seven: with his forehead, palms, knees and feet; and he was forbidden to gather hair or garment."
"2888","From Ibrahim ibn Yazid, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Tawus, he said: I heard Ibn Abbas saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was commanded to prostrate on seven bones, and not to gather hair nor garment. He said: The forehead - then he places his hand upon it, then passes to his nose - and the palms, knees and feet."
"2889","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was commanded to prostrate on seven, and not to gather hair nor garment."
"2890","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was commanded to pray upon seven: upon his palms, knees, toes, and forehead. Then Tawus passed, wiping, when he said: and his forehead, then passed until he wiped his nose; and not to gather hair nor clothes. Ibn Tawus said: I do not know which of the seven his father began with."
"2891","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Those who passed before used to say: A person prostrates upon his face, palms, knees and feet, and does not gather hair nor garment."
"2892","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibrahim ibn Maysarah informed me: He asked Tawus: Is the nose part of the forehead? He said: It is better."
"2893","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: al-Hakam ibn Aban informed me, from Ikrimah, that he said: Place your nose until the haughtiness comes out of it. I said: What is al-ragham? He said: Pride."
"2894","From Simak ibn Harb, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: When you prostrate, press your nose to the ground."
"2895","Mamar informed us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Said al-Khudri: He saw mud on the nose of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from the effect of prostration, and they had been rained upon at night."
"2896","From Israil, from Jabir, he said: I asked al-Shabi about a man prostrating upon his forehead. He said: It suffices him."
"2897","From Mamar, from Asim ibn Sulayman, from Ikrimah, freedman of Ibn Abbas: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a woman prostrating and lifting her nose, and he said a stern word in disapproval of her lifting her nose."
"2898","From al-Thawri, from Asim, from Ikrimah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a man praying, or a woman, and said: Allah does not accept a prayer in which the nose does not touch what the forehead touches."
"2899","From al-Thawri, from Abdullah ibn Isa, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla saw me praying and said: O my son, make your nose touch the ground."
"2900","From al-Thawri, from Wiqa, from Said ibn Jubayr, he said: Prostrate upon your nose."
"2901","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Wiqa informed me, from Said ibn Jubayr, he said: If you do not place your nose along with your forehead, that prostration will not be accepted from you."
"2902","From al-Thawri, from Hisham, from Ibn Sirin: he would prostrate upon his nose."
"2903","From al-Thawri, from Wiqa: He was asked about a man who prostrates upon his forehead but does not prostrate upon his nose; he said: It suffices him."
"2904","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I said to him: Placing the nose along with the forehead? He said: I sometimes prostrate upon it and sometimes I do not prostrate upon it, but that I prostrate upon it is dearer to me."
"2905","From al-Thawri, he said: Some of them used to say: Whoever holds that prostration upon the nose is required, then prostrates upon his forehead and does not prostrate upon his nose, it suffices him; but whoever holds that there is no prostration upon his nose, then prostrates upon the nose and does not prostrate upon the forehead, it does not suffice him."
"2906","From al-Thawri, from Mukhawwal, from a man, from Abu Rafi, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade that a man pray with his head tied up in a knot."
"2907","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Imran ibn Musa narrated to me, from Said ibn Abi Said, from his father, that he saw Abu Rafi, the freedman of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), pass by al-Hasan ibn Ali while al-Hasan was praying standing with his braid stuck into the back of his neck; so Abu Rafi undid it, and he turned toward him angrily. Abu Rafi said to him: Attend to your prayer and do not be angry, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: That is the share of Satan — he said: the seat of Satan — meaning the sticking place of his braid."
"2908","From al-Thawri, from Abu Hashim al-Wasiti, from Mujahid, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab passed by a son of his while he was praying with his head tied up, so he pulled him until he threw him down."
"2909","From al-Hasan ibn Umarah, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not tie up your hair in prayer, for it is the share of Satan."
"2910","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, he said: It is disliked that a man pray with his head tied up, or play with the pebbles, or spit in front of his face or to his right."
"2911","From al-Thawri, from Abu Hashim al-Wasiti, from Mujahid, he said: Hudhayfah passed by a son of his while he was praying with two braids tied up, so he called for a blade and cut one of them, then said: If you wish, do the same with the other, and if you wish, leave it."
"2912","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Zayd ibn Wahb, he said: Abdullah ibn Masud passed by a man prostrating with his head tied up, so he undid it. When he finished, Abdullah said to him: Do not tie up, for your hair prostrates, and for every hair there is a reward. He said: I only tied it up so that it would not get dusty. He said: That it get dusty is better for you."
"2913","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a man prostrating while guarding his hair with his hand, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, wipe away his hair. He said: So his hair fell out. From Mamar, from Qatadah, the same, except that Qatadah said: His head became bald."
"2914","It was narrated to me that Ibn Abbas said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I have been commanded not to gather up hair nor garment. He said: He does not gather the hair away from the ground."
"2915","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I said: I pray in the rain in a cloak of mine while water flows beside me? He said: Do not gather it up. He said: Then it will be ruined. He said: Even so, leave it in the water. Abd al-Razzaq said: And we do not act upon it."
"2916","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A man pulling his cloak from beneath him, then not lifting it from the ground — is his pulling it away gathering up? He said: No harm when he sits; that is only in prostration."
"2917","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I said to him: A man gathers up his hair outside prayer, then the prayer is called; he said: Let him spread his head and let it loose."
"2918","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A desert man — one of them gathers up his hair for a long time because of his standing among his livestock and his work; he said: No harm, for he only gathers it up because of his work; gathering up the hair for prayer is what was forbidden."
"2919","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Is there fear that the turban counts as gathering up the hair? He said: That only comes down to intention."
"2920","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: May a man braid his two side-locks? He said: No harm, for that is done for other than gathering it up for prayer — turbans and braiding the two side-locks."
"2921","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I do not like one with two braids to place his braids, when they grow long, upon his back. He said: Then where? He said: Upon his chest."
"2922","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if I placed my forearms upon the ground and gathered up my hair and my garment? He said: Then do not repeat, and do not prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness."
"2923","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: He raises his head from prostration, then sits until everything in him settles in its place."
"2924","Mamar informed us, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would sometimes raise his head from the prostration and the bow and remain between them until one would say: He has forgotten."
"2925","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, that he used to say between the two prostrations: My Lord, forgive me, have mercy on me, mend me, and provide for me. And Abd al-Razzaq holds this view."
"2926","From Said ibn Abd al-Aziz, that he heard Makhul say between the two prostrations: O Allah, forgive me, have mercy on me, guide me, provide for me, and mend me."
"2927","From Ibn Jurayj and Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, he said: I saw my father remain between the two prostrations."
"2928","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Tawus, he said: My father used to recite much Quran between the two prostrations."
"2929","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, he said: I said to Ibrahim: Do you say anything between the two prostrations? He said: I do not say anything between them."
"2930","From Uthman ibn Matar, from Husayn al-Muallim, from Budayl al-Uqayli, from Abu al-Jawza, from Aishah, she said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raised his head from the prostration, he would not prostrate again until he sat upright."
"2931","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin: That he used to dislike blowing in prayer."
"2932","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, he said: Three blows are disliked: where one prostrates, and a blow upon drink, and a blow upon food."
"2933","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from one who heard Ibn Abbas say: Whoever blows in prayer has spoken."
"2934","From Ibn Uyaynah, from al-Amash, from Muslim ibn Subayh, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Blowing in prayer is like speech."
"2935","From Qays ibn al-Rabi, from Abu Husayn, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: Blowing in prayer is speech."
"2936","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Salih, the freedman of al-Tawamah, that he heard Abu Hurayrah say: None of you should blow in his prayer."
"2937","From Qays ibn al-Rabi, from Abu Husayn, he said: I heard Ibrahim al-Nakhai and Said ibn Jubayr say: Blowing in prayer is speech."
"2938","From Abu Husayn, from Said ibn Jubayr, he said: I do not care whether I blew or spoke."
"2939","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, he said: They used to dislike blowing, because it harms the one sitting beside."
"2940","From Mamar, he said: I asked Ata al-Khurasani and Ayyub about a man who sits on his heels when he raises his head from prostration until he prostrates the other one. Ayyub said: Al-Hasan and Ibn Sirin would not sit on their heels. Ata said: So we used to hear until the people of Makkah came to us with otherwise."
"2941","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: When one of you prays, let him not squat the squatting of the dog."
"2942","From Mamar and Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Khuthaym, from Ibn Labibah, that Abu Hurayrah said to him: Beware the crouching of the dog and squatting, and guard against forgetfulness until you finish the prescribed prayer."
"2943","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, he said: Squatting is the hindmost of Satan."
"2944","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim: That he used to dislike squatting and sitting on the side with one leg out."
"2945","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father: That he saw Ibn Umar, Ibn al-Zubayr, and Ibn Abbas squatting between the two prostrations."
"2946","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, he said: It is of the Sunnah that your heel touch your buttocks in prayer between the two prostrations."
"2947","From Amr ibn Hawshab, he said: Abdullah ibn Abi Yazid informed me that he saw Umar and Ibn Umar squatting between the two prostrations."
"2948","From Amr ibn Hawshab, he said: Ikrimah informed me that he heard Ibn Abbas say: Squatting in prayer is the Sunnah."
"2949","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, from Tawus, he said: I heard Ibn Abbas say: It is of the Sunnah that your heel touch your buttocks."
"2950","He said: Tawus said: And I saw the Abadilah squatting — Ibn Umar, Ibn Abbas, and Ibn al-Zubayr."
"2951","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me that he saw Ibn Umar do two things in the first prostration of the even and the odd: He said: I saw him squat once, kneeling upon the tips of both his feet together, and once fold his left leg and spread it, sitting upon it, while the right one he keeps upright, bending it upon the tips of all his toes. He said: I saw him do that in the first of the two prostrations, and in the third prostration of the odd, then he stands firm and rises."
"2952","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Tawus say: We said to Ibn Abbas about squatting upon the feet? He said: It is the Sunnah. We said: We see it as harshness toward a man. Ibn Abbas said: Rather, it is the Sunnah of your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2953","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: I asked him about sitting in prayer in the two-rakah sitting; he said: He folds the left under the right."
"2954","From al-Thawri, from al-Zubayr, from Ibrahim, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to spread his left leg until the outside of it would be seen blackened."
"2955","From al-Thawri, from Asim ibn Kulayb, from his father, from Wail ibn Hujr, he said: I watched the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in prayer, and when he sat, he spread his left leg."
"2956","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I saw Ibn Umar sit in the two-rakah sitting, sitting upon his left, spreading it and sitting upon it, and squatting upon the toes of his right, kneeling upon it, with them coming behind him upon all its toes. From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi informed me, from Ibn Umar, the same as the report of Ata."
"2957","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, he said: Ibn Umar sat cross-legged in his prayer, then said: It is not of the Sunnah of prayer, but my leg complains."
"2958","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard a man ask Ata: Was it liked for a man to sit upon his left foot in prayer? He said: Yes."
"2959","From Malik, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from Abdullah ibn Abdullah ibn Umar, he said: Ibn Umar prayed cross-legged, so I did that while I was young in age. He said: Why do you do that? He said: I said: For you do it. He said: It is not of the Sunnah of prayer, but the Sunnah of prayer is that you fold the left and set up the right. Abdullah said: My legs do not carry me."
"2960","From Malik, from Sadaqah ibn Yasar, from al-Mughirah ibn Hakim, that he saw Ibn Umar cross-legged in two prostrations of the prayer upon the tops of his feet; he mentioned that to him, and he said: It is not of the Sunnah of prayer, but I do that because I complain."
"2961","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, he said: The Sunnah in sitting in prayer is that you fold the left and squat upon the right."
"2962","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Ibn Talhah, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Ata, from Abu Humayd al-Saidi: That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he sat in prayer in the first two rakahs, would set up his right foot and spread the left, and point with the finger next to the thumb; and when he sat in the last two, he would bring his seat to the ground and set up his right foot."
"2963","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin: He would spread his left leg and squat upon the right. He said: And al-Hasan would spread the right for the left."
"2964","From Malik and Ibn Uyaynah, from Muslim ibn Abi Maryam, from Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari, he said: I prayed beside Ibn Umar while turning over pebbles in prayer; when he finished, he said: Turning over pebbles in prayer is of Satan, but as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to do — when he sat in prayer, he placed his left palm upon his left thigh and his right palm upon his right thigh, clenched his fingers, and pointed with the finger next to the thumb."
"2965","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Khalid informed me: It reached me from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that when he sat in the two-rakah sitting, he would lay the left inward and sit upon it, placing his foot under his buttock until the back of his foot blackened from the gravel ground."
"2966","From Uthman ibn Matar, from al-Husayn al-Muallim, from Budayl al-Uqayli, from Abu al-Jawza, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would spread his leg — or he said: his left foot — for the right. He said: And she used to forbid us the hindmost of Satan."
"2967","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata used to complain of his left leg, so he would put out the right while his left was drawn in, gathering it upright. I said: Why not sit cross-legged? He said: I dislike that. I said: What if I sat cross-legged or stretched my leg before me in prayer? He said: Prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness."
"2968","From al-Thawri and Ibn Uyaynah, from Husayn, from Haytham ibn Shihab, he said: Abdullah said: That I sit upon two hot stones is better than that I sit in prayer cross-legged."
"2969","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Do you sit cross-legged between the two rakahs while you are young? He said: Yes. I said: I want to sit cross-legged before the tashahhud. He said: Do not do it until you recite the tashahhud; when you have recited it, then sit cross-legged, or sit with knees drawn up, or do what you wish. If you do it before the tashahhud in the prescribed prayer, prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness; as for voluntary prayer, if you do it, do not prostrate. He said: And dearer to me is that you recite the tashahhud laying your left inward beneath you and setting up the other, squatting upon it with its toes in the dust, like the sitting of Ibn Umar. I said: So I place my left hand likewise before the tashahhud? He said: No, and I do not like that."
"2970","From Mamar, from Ismail ibn Umayyah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade that a man sit in prayer leaning upon his hands."
"2971","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi informed me that Ibn Umar saw a man sitting leaning upon his hands, and said: What makes you sit in your prayer the sitting of those who earned anger?"
"2972","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar: That he saw a man sitting leaning with his hand upon the ground, and said: You have sat the sitting of a people who were punished."
"2973","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibrahim ibn Maysarah informed me that he heard Amr ibn al-Sharid report from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he used to say about a man placing his left when he sits in prayer: It is the sitting of those who earned anger."
"2974","From al-Thawri, from Dawud, from Abu al-Aliyah, he said: Ibn Abbas heard a man, when he sat in prayer, say: Praise be to Allah, before the tashahhud, so he rebuked him, saying: Begin with the tashahhud."
"2975","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, he said: I heard Tawus say: I know nothing after the two rakahs except the tashahhud."
"2976","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: The first two-rakah sitting is only for the tashahhud, and the last is for supplication and longing, and the last is the longer of the two."
"2977","From al-Thawri, from Hammad and Mansur and Husayn and al-Amash and Abu Hisham, from Abu Wail, and from Abu Ishaq, from al-Aswad, and Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, he said: We did not know what to say in prayer, so we used to say: Peace be upon Allah, peace be upon Gabriel, peace be upon Michael. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught us and said: Do not say: Peace be upon Allah, for Allah is Peace. When you sit in two rakahs, say: Greetings belong to Allah, and prayers and good words. Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah. Abu Wail said in the hadith of Abdullah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): When you say it, it reaches every righteous servant in the heaven and on earth. And Abu Ishaq said in the hadith of Abdullah: When you say it, it reaches every near angel or sent prophet or righteous servant. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger."
"2978","My father informed me, from Ibrahim, he said: Rabi ibn Khuthaym came to Alqamah seeking his counsel to add therein: And His forgiveness. Alqamah said: We only stop at what we were taught."
"2979","From Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Ibn Masud, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was taught the openings of good and its comprehensive ones — or the comprehensive of good and its seals — and we did not know what to say in our prayer until he taught us. He said: Say: Greetings belong to Allah, and prayers and good words. Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger."
"2980","From Mamar, from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, from Shaqiq ibn Salamah, from Abdullah ibn Masud, he said: We used to pray with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the people would say: Peace be upon Allah, peace be upon Gabriel, peace be upon Michael, peace be upon the near angels. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not say: Peace be upon Allah, for Allah is Peace. He said: So he taught them the tashahhud and said: Say: Greetings belong to Allah, and prayers and good words. Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger."
"2981","From Mamar, from Qatadah, from Yunus ibn Jubayr, from Hittan ibn Abdullah al-Raqashi, that Abu Musa led his companions in a prayer. He said: When he sat, a man among the people said: The prayer is established with righteousness and zakah. He said: When Abu Musa finished his prayer, he said: Which of you said such-and-such words? The people fell silent. Abu Musa said: O Hittan, perhaps you said it? He said: I said: No, by Allah, I did not say it, and I feared you would blame me for it. A man among the people said: I said it, and I meant only good by it. Abu Musa said: Do you not know how your prayer is? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us and made clear to us the ways, and taught us our prayer, and said: When you pray, straighten your rows, then let one of you lead you. When he says takbir, say takbir; and when he says: Not of those who earned anger nor of the astray, say: Amen, Allah will answer you. And when he says takbir and bows, say takbir and bow, for the imam bows before you and rises before you. The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: That for that. And when he says: Allah hears whoever praises Him, say: Our Lord, to You belongs praise, Allah will hear you, for He decreed upon the tongue of His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Allah hears whoever praises Him. And when at the sitting, let one of you say, the first thing he sits: Greetings belong to Allah, good words; prayers belong to Allah. Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger."
"2982","From Mamar, from one who heard al-Hasan say: Greetings belong to Allah, good words; prayers belong to Allah. Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger."
"2983","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari, he said: I witnessed Umar ibn al-Khattab teaching the tashahhud, and he said: Greetings belong to Allah, pure things belong to Allah, good things belong to Allah. Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger. Abd al-Razzaq said: And Mamar used to hold this, and I hold it. From Ibn Jurayj, from Urwah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari, from Umar, the same as the hadith of Mamar, except that he said: And the mercy of Allah; peace be upon us. From Mamar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, the same as the hadith of al-Zuhri, except that he used to say at its beginning: In the name of Allah, best of names, and put in place of pure things: blessed ones."
"2984","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I heard Ibn Abbas and Ibn al-Zubayr say in the tashahhud in prayer: Blessed greetings belong to Allah; prayers, good things belong to Allah. Peace be upon the Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger. He said: I heard Ibn al-Zubayr say them upon the pulpit, teaching them to the people. He said: And I heard Ibn Abbas say them likewise. I said: Did Ibn Abbas and Ibn al-Zubayr not differ therein? He said: No."
"2985","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Tawus informed me, from his father: That he used to say in the tashahhud: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate; blessed greetings and prayers, good words belong to Allah. Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger. Tawus said about the tashahhud: It used to be taught as the Quran is taught. From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sulayman al-Ahwal informed me, from Tawus, about the tashahhud as Ibn Tawus informed me, except that he did not put therein: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate. Abd al-Rahman said: I mentioned that to Said ibn Jubayr, and he said: Tawus has retracted part of it. So I informed Tawus of that, and he denied that he had retracted anything of it, and said: Even if I only heard Abdullah ibn Abbas once or twice."
"2986","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Nafi: How did Ibn Umar recite the tashahhud? He said: He used to say: In the name of Allah; greetings belong to Allah; prayers belong to Allah; pure things belong to Allah. Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah. Then he would testify: I bore witness that there is no god but Allah; I bore witness that there is no god but Allah; I bore witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, following them up with the salutation."
"2987","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim: He would not say salam in the first two-rakah sitting; he used to consider that a breaking of his prayer. Al-Zuhri said: As for me, I say salam."
"2988","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: That the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was alive, used to say salam: Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. When he died, they said: Peace be upon the Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings."
"2989","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: While the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was teaching the tashahhud, a man said: And I bear witness that Muhammad is His Messenger and His servant. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was a servant before I was a messenger. Say: And I bear witness that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger."
"2990","From Mamar, from Khusayf al-Jazari, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in sleep; he came to me, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, they differed over the tashahhud for us; so-and-so said such, and so-and-so said such, and Ibn Masud said such. He said: The Sunnah is the Sunnah of Ibn Masud."
"2991","From Mamar and Qatadah and Hammad, about a man who forgot the tashahhud at the end of his prayer until he left: They said: He does not repeat; his prayer is complete."
"2992","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: No prescribed prayer nor voluntary except with tashahhud. I said: I forgot the tashahhud in the dawn prayer. He said: Do not repeat, and do not prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness, but recite the tashahhud when you remember."
"2993","From Abdullah ibn Kathir, from Shubah, from Muslim al-Shami, from Hamalah, a man of Akk, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: No prayer is valid except with tashahhud."
"2994","From Uthman ibn Matar, from Husayn, from Budayl al-Uqayli, from Abu al-Jawza, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say the greetings between every two rakahs."
"2995","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Umayr ibn Sad, from Ibn Masud, that he used to teach them the tashahhud, then say: O Allah, I ask You of all good, what I know of it and what I do not know, and I seek refuge in You from all evil, what I know of it and what I do not know. O Allah, I ask You of the best of what Your righteous servants asked You, and I seek refuge in You from the evil of what Your righteous servants sought refuge from. O Allah, our Lord, give us good in this world and good in the Hereafter and shield us from the punishment of the Fire. Our Lord, forgive us our sins and expiate from us our evil deeds and take us with the righteous. Our Lord, and give us what You promised us upon Your messengers and do not disgrace us on the Day of Resurrection; indeed You do not break the promise."
"2996","From al-Thawri, from Abu al-Jahhaf, from al-Harith ibn Yazid, from Ibrahim, he said: I asked him about the imam; he said: He says takbir, then says: Glory be to You, O Allah, and with Your praise, then seeks refuge silently, and recites: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate, silently, then recites aloud: Praise be to Allah, Lord of the worlds. Then he says takbir while going down, then stands and says takbir while going down, then sits in the first two for the tashahhud and does not add to it; and in the last two, the tashahhud and five comprehensive words. Al-Thawri said: Mansur informed me; he said: I said to Ibrahim: The prayer upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: They would not add to them."
"2997","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Is the prayer upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) not with the tashahhud? He said: Nothing is added to the tashahhud in what is known of the tashahhud, except that a man may say after the tashahhud what he wishes."
"2998","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: The tashahhud suffices you for the prayer upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2999","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that he used to say after the tashahhud in the last two-rakah sitting words he would teach strictly. He said: I seek refuge in Allah from the punishment of Hell, and I seek refuge in Allah from the evil of the False Messiah, and I seek refuge in Allah from the punishment of the grave, and I seek refuge in Allah from the trial of life and death. He said: He used to teach them and report them from Aishah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: He said to a man: Did you say them in your prayer? He said: No. He said: Then repeat your prayer — meaning this saying."
"3000","From Mamar ibn Rashid, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: O Allah, I seek refuge in You from the punishment of the grave, and from the trial of life and death, and from the evil of the trial of the False Messiah."
"3001","From al-Thawri, from Hisham, from Muhammad, he said: When he recited the tashahhud, he would cast his cloak after the tashahhud in the last rakah."
"3002","From Muqatil, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, he said: Whoever catches a rakah with the imam, or misses a rakah, let him not recite the tashahhud with the imam, but let him declare the oneness until he stands. That was mentioned to al-Thawri, and he said: In every sitting there is a tashahhud."
"3003","From Malik, he said: I asked Nafi and Ibn Shihab about that, and they said: He recites the tashahhud."
"3004","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: He recites the tashahhud."
"3005","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If you are sitting in an odd one while the imam is in an even, then recite the tashahhud and do not say salam. Say: Greetings belong to Allah; blessed ones belong to Allah; prayers, good things belong to Allah; and glorify, and leave the salam, and recite the tashahhud thus. I said: Do I glorify and declare oneness and say takbir? He said: No — if you wish."
"3006","From Qays ibn al-Rabi, from Mansur, from Abu Mashar, from Ibrahim, he said: He does not recite the tashahhud."
"3007","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: He does not recite the tashahhud."
"3008","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, he said: When Ibn Umar had an odd one while the imam was in an even, he would not say salam in his tashahhud; he used to consider it a breaking of his prayer. Mamar said: Al-Zuhri said: And I testify and say salam in my tashahhud."
"3009","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If you miss a rakah with the imam and he sits, then recite the tashahhud in an even while you are in an odd; when the imam finishes, complete what remains of your prayer, then prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness. I said: Why would I prostrate them? He said: Because one does not sit in an odd nor recite the tashahhud therein, and because he sat in an odd. I said: Does that count from him as forgetfulness and mistake? He said: Yes."
"3010","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Muslim ibn Musabbih ibn al-Zubayr said: He said: Ibn al-Zubayr missed two rakahs of noon; when the imam said salam, Ibn al-Zubayr stood and completed the rakah, and when he said salam, he prostrated the two prostrations of forgetfulness. From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, from Abu Said al-Khudri and Ibn Umar, that they used to do that. Ibn Jurayj said: And I was informed, after Ata died, that he transmitted the hadith of Ibn Umar, from Ismail ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Dhuayb al-Asadi."
"3011","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi informed me; he said: I saw Ibn Umar miss a rakah, and he sat in an odd while the imam was in an even; when the imam said salam, he stood and completed what remained upon him, then prostrated the two prostrations of forgetfulness. From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, the same."
"3012","Al-Zuhri said: And Said ibn al-Musayyib informed me, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the prayer is called, do not come to it running, but come to it walking, with calmness upon you. What you catch, pray; and what you miss, complete. He did not mention his prostration."
"3013","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad, from Amr ibn Hazm, from a man among the Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: O Allah, bless Muhammad and the household, and his wives and his offspring, as You blessed Ibrahim and the family of Ibrahim; indeed You are Praiseworthy, Glorious. And bless Muhammad and his household, and his wives and his offspring, as You blessed Ibrahim and the family of Ibrahim; indeed You are Praiseworthy, Glorious. Ibn Tawus said: And my father used to say the same."
"3014","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, that he used to say: O Allah, accept the great intercession of Muhammad, and raise his highest rank, and give him his request in the Hereafter and the first life, as You gave Ibrahim and Musa. And Mamar would sometimes mention it, from Ibn Tawus, from Ikrimah ibn Khalid, from Ibn Abbas."
"3015","From Abdullah ibn Muharrar, from al-Hakam, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Kab ibn Ujrah; and al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from al-Hakam, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Kab ibn Ujrah, that a man said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): O Messenger of Allah, we know how to greet you, so how do we bless you? He said: Say: O Allah, bless Muhammad and the family of Muhammad, as You blessed Ibrahim; indeed You are Praiseworthy, Glorious. O Allah, bless Muhammad as You blessed Ibrahim; indeed You are Praiseworthy, Glorious."
"3016","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Abi Layla narrated to me, from Kab ibn Ujrah, he said: I was sitting with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when a man came to him and said: We know how to greet you, so how do we bless you? He said: Say: O Allah, bless Muhammad and the family of Muhammad, as You blessed the family of Ibrahim; indeed You are Praiseworthy, Glorious."
"3017","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Kab ibn Ujrah, he said: O Messenger of Allah, we know how to greet you, so how do we bless you? He said: Say: O Allah, bless the family of Muhammad; O Allah, bless the family of Muhammad, as You blessed and blessed Ibrahim and the family of Ibrahim; indeed You are Praiseworthy, Glorious."
"3018","From Malik, from Nuaym ibn Abdullah al-Mujmir, the freedman of Umar ibn al-Khattab, that Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Zayd informed him, from Abu Masud al-Ansari, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us and sat with us in the gathering of Sad ibn Ubadah. Bashir ibn Sad — he is the father of al-Numan ibn Bashir — said to him: Allah commanded us to bless you, so how do we bless you? He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fell silent until we wished he had not asked him. Then he said: Say: O Allah, bless Muhammad and the family of Muhammad, as You blessed Ibrahim; and bless Muhammad and the family of Muhammad, as You blessed the family of Ibrahim among the worlds; indeed You are Praiseworthy, Glorious. And the greeting is as you know."
"3019","From al-Thawri, from Abu Salamah, from Awn ibn Abdullah, from a man, from al-Aswad ibn Yazid, from Ibn Masud, that he used to say: O Allah, place Your blessings and Your mercy and Your benedictions upon the master of the messengers and the leader of the God-fearing and the seal of the prophets, Muhammad, Your servant and Your Messenger, the leader of good and the guide of good and the messenger of mercy. O Allah, raise him to a praised station envied by the first and the last. O Allah, bless Muhammad and the family of Muhammad, as You blessed the family of Ibrahim; indeed You are Praiseworthy, Glorious. O Allah, bless Muhammad and the family of Muhammad, as You blessed the family of Ibrahim; indeed You are Praiseworthy, Glorious."
"3020","From al-Thawri — and I heard him when a man asked him about His saying: O Allah, bless Muhammad and the family of Muhammad — he said: They differed about them: among them are those who said: The family of Muhammad are his household; and among them are those who say: Whoever obeys him."
"3021","From Mamar, from Yunus ibn Khabbab, from Mujahid, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You are presented to me with your names and your marks, so bless me well."
"3022","From al-Thawri, from Abu Salamah, from Awn ibn Abdullah, from a man, from al-Aswad ibn Yazid, from Ibn Masud, he said: When you pray, bless your Prophet well."
"3023","From Mamar, from Aban, from Anas, from Abu Talhah, he said: I entered upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) one day and found him joyful. I said: O Messenger of Allah, I do not know when I saw you with better cheer and sweeter spirit than today? He said: And what prevents me, when Gabriel just left me and gave me glad tidings that for every servant who blesses me with a blessing, ten good deeds are written for him, ten evil deeds are wiped from him, ten degrees are raised for him, it is presented to me as he said it, and he is answered with the like of what he supplicated."
"3024","From Ibn Uyaynah, he said: Yaqub ibn Zayd al-Taymi informed me; he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A comer from my Lord came to me and said: No servant blesses you with a blessing except that Allah blesses him tenfold. He said: A man said: O Messenger of Allah, shall I not make half my supplication for you? He said: If you wish. He said: Shall I not make all my supplication for you? He said: Then Allah will suffice you the concern of this world and the Hereafter."
"3025","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabiah, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever blesses me with a blessing, Allah blesses him; so increase or lessen."
